      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE RINGS OF SATURN.</p>
        <p>The rings of Saturn are thin plane sheets, most probably less than 100 miles in thick- ness, though 168,000 miles in diameter. They do not, however, form an unbroken sheet, but are compcsad of three divisions an outer one, about 10,000 miles wide, separated from the second by a dark space •of 1,600 miles; a second, about 16,000 miles in width, brighter than the first; -and this shades gra- dually into the third, which is faint and partly transparent; doubtless because the particles which compose it are so far apart that we can see through between them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>260Dr0. COLDER THAN ICE!</head>
        <p>260Dr0. COLDER THAN ICE!</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting and important facts connected with the liquefying of refrac- tory gases is the low temperatures attained. Various considerations have led to the fixing of 273 deg. below zero on the Centigrade scale as the absolute zero of temperature, the total absence of all heat. In liquefying hydrogen temperatures not far removed from this abso- lute zero are attained. Thus the boiling point of liquid hydrogen is 232cleg. below zero, or 21deg. of absolute temperature. Solid hydrogen gives a temperature of 17deg., and by exhaustion a temperature of 13deg. above absolute zero may be reached. This, the Globe says, is the lowest temperature hitherto attained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ELECTRIC LIGHT AND EYESIGHT.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT AND EYESIGHT.</p>
        <p>It is regrettable, says an engineering corre- spondent of the T imes, that there should still be erroneous opinions in circulation concern- ing the alleged unfavourable effects of elec- tric light on weak eyes. One of the lady assistant inspectors of factories states that "over and over again I have noticed the strained look• in the eyes of workers who have to use the electric light for many hours at a time, or in rooms where the lights are not sufficiently screened." The truth is, says the writer, that it is the very convenience of electric lighting which is thus turned to its disparagement. An electric lamp can be placed almost anywhere without danger, and as a natural consequence it is very frequently put in a position dictated rather by considera- tions of high illuminating power than of the worker's eyesight.</p>
        <p>HOW INSECTS SEE.</p>
        <p>The great difference between the com- pound eye of the insect, wita its many lenses, and our own suggests that they see in a dif- ferent manner from us. Yet the theory of the origin of flowers by the selective action of in- sects implies that they perceive the form and colour of the flowers much as we do. Experi- ments and observations carried out inde- pendently by Exner and F. Plauteau agree in the conclusion that the sense of sight in in- sects differs much from that in man. Insects, they assert, see shape badly. but, on the other hand, have a keen perception of motion. The following observation on wasps seems to support the above view of vision in insects. A number of wasps were hawking the flies which settled on a notice- board affixed to a wall. On this board were visible the black heads of several nails about the same size as the flies. The wasps made repeated dashes at the flies, and several of them made similar dashes at the nail heads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IMMORTALITY OF THE SOUL.</head>
        <p>IMMORTALITY OF THE SOUL.</p>
        <p>Sir Oliver Lodge says that the simple important truth to be kept in sight is the commonplace fact that there is nothing im- mortal or persistent about the body except the material atoms of which it is composed. Ile dismisses utterly the notion that these satoms will some day be gathered and re- smiled so as to constitute a complete man as lie appeared on the earth, and who, there- fore, will last for ever. Sir Oliver Gays that nobody knows what the soul is, but that com- mon sense rebels against -it being nothing, and that no genuine science has assumed to declare it a purely imaginary nonentity. He holds it must be acknowledged by science that no really existing thing perishes; it only changes form. As this has been shewn clearly in the ease of matter and energy, it must also be true of mind, consciousness, will, memory, love, and other activities which interact with matter and appeal to the bodily senses. These facts of the indiyidual human consciousness, he says, cannot be regarded as flouting, and they will never vanish into nothingness. They did not arise with us. They never sprang sud- denly into being from previous non-exist- ence. They are eternal, ad will in eternal being endure for ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>DEEP. WATER DIVING.</head>
        <p>DEEP. WATER DIVING.</p>
        <p>The Admiralty Committee on Deep Water Diving was appointed in August, 1905. A re- port was completed and presented to the Ad- miralty at the end of 1906, but its publication has been delayed pending the completion of a further series of experiments carried out at the Lister Institute. It is a most interesting document, says the Engineer, and proves that diving operations can be carried out with safety in depths up to 210 ft. provided proper precautions are adopted and -suitable ap- pliances used. Both Lieutenant Damant and Mr. Catto descended to that depth off the coast of Scotland, and experienced no ill re- sults. The Committee report that the respira- tory distress which, under the conditions of diving hitherto obtaining, usually prevents divers from working at more than very mode- rate depths, such as fifteen to twenty fathoms, is due to the PRESSURE OF CARBON DIOXIDE</p>
        <p>in the air i.n the helmet, and can be entirely obviated by increasing the supply of air in direct proportion to the increase in the abso- lute pressure. To enable a sufficient supply of air to be delivered to the divers two or more pumps should be coupled together when operations, are being carried out in depths ex- ceeding eighteen fathoms. The serious dan- gers arising from the liberation of bubbles of nitrogen in the blood and tissues on the ascent of a diver from deep water can best be met by (1) limiting the time spent in deep water; and (2) ascending most of the distance rapidly, and afterwards making the last part of the ascent in stages, with stoppages inter- posed. The Committee recommend that fur- tiler experiments on certain points be carried out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
