      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SOUNDING A VOLCANO.</head>
        <p>SOUNDING A VOLCANO.</p>
        <p>The perils of scientific investigation are many and various, ranging from death from poisonous germs to the loss of an eye by the explcsion of a liquid air vacuum vessel—both of which catastrophes have occurred in Lon- don during recent years. But no form of scientific inquiry is fraught with more dan- ger than that of the vulcanologist, and a vivid description of a dangerous experience has been given in the World's Work in an article by Mr. Frank Perret, of the Observa- tory of Vesuvius: "We had an adventure on Vesuvius the mere remembrance of which makes me shudder. This time the gases were our foe—gases and ashes blown down from the crater by an easterly gale. Fifty persons —men, women, and c hildren—were gathered in with great difficulty to the barracks, which gave inadequate protection, and the problem was how to reach the Observatory. This was only sixty yards away, but the gases made the air almost unbreathable, and the ashes produced a</p>
        <p>DARKNESS THAT WAS ABSOLUTE. We could not see our bands before our faces. If the eyes were opened they filled at once with sand and ashes, which were driven by the blast with such force as to make the lips bleed. Only sixty yards to go, but the road was covered deep with ashes as a desert is covered with sand, and, of course, no com- pass could be used, for it could not be seen. The fifty were roped together, and, with one and anchored to the barracks door, the head of this human snake circled about until HOMO familiar spot was found; then the tail drew up, an,d the manceuvre was repeated until the Observatory gate was reached. Once in- side, more dead than alive, every expedient to absorb the gases and to manufacture oxygen was considered, but no materials were to hand. We were forced to endure for eight hours those deadly gases, and an atmo- sphere of floating ashes so thick that a lamp could not be seen across the room. But at naidnight the blast subsided, and the gases v:ere dissipated. Of the fifty refugees, all bet one young man survived the terrible ordeal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RESULTS OF INFLUENZA.</head>
        <p>RESULTS OF INFLUENZA.</p>
        <p>Heart weakness and pains cured by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Morgan, of 146, Villiers-street, Brook- street, Preston, writes :—" Some time ago I had a severe attack of Influenza, which left me with Rheu- matic Fever. I was bedfast three weeks, and when I got up I was helpless and the doctor said I had developed a weak heart. The pains were terrible and despite many so called remedies I grew worse, and if I tried to do any work I felt as if 1 were going to faint. I tried all sorts of medicines, but none did rue any good till I tried Dr. Cassell's Tablets. I took two before meals for a month and began to feel quite myself again, and now I am glad to say I am quite cured and have felt no pains since." Dr. Cassell's Tablets are the finest invigorator and bodily restorer extant, and are invaluable in cases of weakness and depression, nerve troubles, storeach and kidney disorders, and all forms of nerve and physical exhaustion. Price 100., Is. I,ld. and 2s. 9d. (containing nearly four times the 101 d. size), of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BAND OF..BOPE.CONCERT,-Thero_,AaA a large</p>
        <p>audience at the Wesleyan School on Monday eveni;g when the closing concert of the Session in connection with the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope, was held. Mrs. James Dodds, president of the Warrington Band of Hope Union, presiding. The proceedings were opened with prayer by the Rev. J. S. Hughes, who afterwards introduced Mrs. Dodds to the meeting. Mrs. Dodds, who was cordially. received, expressed her great pleasure to visit the various bands of hope in the district, and appealed to all to do what lay in their power for the cause of tem- perance. In a brief reference to the war now raging over the Licensing Bill she exhorted all temperance people to work and pray that it might become law. Miss Elsie Whitfield presented Mrs. Dodds with a beautiful bouquet of orchids on behalf of the Band of Hope. A good programme was gone through opened by a well rendered pianoforte duet by the Misses Violet Brown and May Hartree. Miss Dolly Cain sang "The new kingdom " very sweetly. Miss May isher.vood was beard to great advantage in " The swallows" and was loudly applauded. The humorous element was in the hands of Master Lawton, the popular boy humorist, who well sustained his reputation and was repeatedly encored. Miss Violet Brown, who possesses a rich and powerful voice, contributed two songs and was deservedly encored, whilst the mandeline selections given by Miss Broornhead contributed very much to the evening's enjoyment and were much appreciated. A number of selected girls trained by Miss May Isher- wood and Miss Edith Hamblett performed a sash drill which was very effectively done and was much appre- ciated. The cantata "The Court of Queen Summer- gold" by pupils of Miss Lloyd Williams was well given, the graceful manner and dainty dresses of the children being much admired. Recitations by Miss A. and M. Mounfield and Thomas Richmond were well given, while the humorous dialogues "How we cured a drunken husband" and" Washing done here "provoked roars of laughter. During the evening medals were awarded to those members who had attained the age of 13 and had been members for three years, and those members who were awarded similar medals last year bad a bar attached to their medals which denotes another year's faithful membership, an addition to be added every year these members continue their membership. A resolution in favour of the Licensing Bill, proposed by Rev. J. S. Hughes, and seconded by Mr. A. Jones, was unanimously passed, to be forwarded to Mr. A. 3. King, M.P., for the Knutsford division, calling open him to support it. The Misses Brown, Hartree and Barber, and Master Whitfield acted as accompanists.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild. At Monday evening's meeting of this guild an innovation was introduced, in the shape of the first visit of the Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild. A devotional service was conducted by the visiting society, practically all the members taking part. Short ',ape's cn How I can best serve Christ ?" were given by the male members, the titles being " How I can best serve Christ as a devotionalleader</p>
        <p>"as a church member" "as a temperance 'worker" and "as a member of the Wesley Guild." The pa per on temperance work was particularly enjoyable, it being pointed out how essential it was that the children should be taught the principles of temperance and total abstin- ence. Miss Hardy sang sweetly " Jean, lover of my soul." - Mr. Mitchell proposed a vote of thanks to the visitors, and said he looked upon the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church as a branch from the Bewsey-road Church.—Mrs Pitt, in seconding, referred to the Licensing Bill, and said that all lovers of sobriety and temperance should give it their support. She hoped the Guild members would send postcards to their Parliamentary representative, and show him they were in favour of the Bill, thus giving him moral support.</p>
        <p>Headache and Constipation generally go together, for the simple reason that the one is the natural outcome of the other. Constipation fills your blood with impurities which obstruct the circulation, poison your system, and that is why you have headache. MOTHER SEIGEL'S SYRUP removes the cause and so restores health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Learning to Ride Early.—Directly King Alfonso's little legs were able to cross a pony, he was given his first riding lessons, his steed "Curly " being no larger than a Newfoundland gog. Up to the time of his death, which occurred only recently " Curly " remained the pet of his Royal master and the Royal stables,. and was as full of tricks as though he had been born an a circus. A somewhat similar freak of pony-nature has now been secured for the young Prince of the Asturias, and this is to commence its training at once, so as to be in readiness for the young Prince's use in a few month's time. An important point in the education of a Spanish prince, and one that is evidently not going ito be neglected n the case of Alfonso Pio Christino Eduardo, is, that he should be taught to ride as early as possible. The Devonshire Dukedom.—The new Duke and Duchess of Devonshire, hitherto Mr. Victor and Lady Evelyn Cavendish, have a young family of two sons and four daughters. The elder boy, now Marquis of Hartington, and heir to the dukedom is thirteen years of age, and the youngest Lady Rachel Cavendish is Six. The Duchess, who is the elder of the two daughters of Lord and Lady Lansdowne, is devoted to her children, and their care and education take up most of her time. She has been accustomed to living very quietly at Holker Hall, One of the seven magnificent seats of the Cavendishes. Chatsworth, the greatest of these, owes most of its glories to the sixth Duke, who succeeded in 1811, and died in 1858, but Bolton Abbey in Yorkshire was the favourite residence of the late Duke. His death has thrown many prominent families into mourning, and the London season will suffer to some extent by their retirement Irons Society entertainment. Ilesse Darmstadt.—Darmstadt where the Prince and Princess of Wales have been staying for a few days is the capital of one of the small grand-duchy's of Germany, and it still retains an affectionate remem- brance of Princess Alice, who was the Grand Duchess from 1862 to the time of her death in 1878. Queen Victoria frequently visited Darmstadt, On the last occasion in April 1884, to attend the marriage of her eldest grand-daughter, Princess Victoria, to Prince Louis of Battenberg. Whilst Queen Victoria was stay- ing under his roof, on that occasion, the Grand Duke— the windower of Princess Alice—married a lady of no , repute. He died in 1892, but the late Queen never forgave him, or again visited Darmstadt after 1884. A Royal Example.—The Princess of Wales is generally supposed to be the best dressed woman in our I Islands, and yet Her Royal Highness is known to spend less upon her wardrobe than many "smart" Society women. One point always insisted upon by the Princess is that the fabrics and trimmings of which all garments are composed should be of home manufacture. England, Scotland, Ireland, Wales, and the Islands. also the Colonies are all patronised, but exorbitant prices are never entertained for a moment. The same rules have been extended to Her Royal Highness's nurseries, the outfits there being of the simplest possible. Many a wealthy American mother would look with scorn upon the plain garments destined to be worn by the children of the Heir Apparent. Then again, the "command" to send in a bill, promptly follows the delivery of all goods to the Princess Of Wales,and every account is settled within three months. Her Royal Highness sets in these ways, as in so many others, examples which Society women generally would do more than well to follow.</p>
        <p>Charming Straw Creations.—Many and wonderful are the users to which milliners are putting straw this season. For useful hats, the bands and bows are mostly carried out in straw, and where ribbon bows are used, these are edged with a band of straw which keeps them from getting out of order. For more dressy headgear, straw flowers are used, and straw "aigrettes," which are most realistic,and very durable. Then we have the new straw " beads," which are usually about the size of cherries, and arranged round the base of the crown, And last but by no means least, we have the straw "'flower " buckles,which give the impression of massed forget-me-nots, button roses, pinks, violets,daisies,and buttercups. For travelling and knock-about wear, these pretty straw trimmings are indeed charming acquisitions. Veils and Ruffles.—The huge floating veil is gone, and this is best so, as the mode was only suitable for the tall, slender flgure,which just managed to "carry it off." The average woman looked all heid, and out of all proportion, in the flowing veil, which to make matters worse was very often be-ruched and be-frilled about the edge. The new veils are generally in two colours, the net of one and the spots of another, the colours being in evidence upon the hat. These veils are slightly gathered along the two edges, many women using tine round elastic for the purpose. Then when the veil is adjusted the fulness can be adjusted over the face, the bag-like arrangement that results proving highly satis- factory. Full ruffles of net to match accompany many of the new veils, the ends being finished with long streamers of velvet ribbon, or ropes of chenille. Queer Pets —Women who devote their affections. to clogs and horses, invariably receive affection and devotion in return. There are many women however who are unwilling to lavish affection without hope of receiving any return. This may account for such pets as a lizard, a tortoise, an owl, a rat and a snake. Monkeys are often adopted by women as pets, but they are not very often a success. As a rule they prove themselves treacherous, and the same may be said, sooner or later, of panthers, baby lions, and baby tigers. One well-known Society woman makes pets of a couple of hens, allowing them to follow her into the house and create nests, and sit upon their ezgc, just as they will. Yet another makes a pet of a huge black beetle, and this curious individual declares that " Petit " understands her quite well, and is devoted to her. Whether " Petit would prefer her attractions to that of freedom, is not however put to the test. The Hon. John Collier and his Pictures.—Artists and critics are already discussing the principal pictures that will be seen at the lioyal Academy this year. and it seems that the public will be disappointed if it is looking for a fresh problem picture from the Hon. John Collier. His forthcoming principal exhibit presents no mystery, but tells its own story in a, very direct and effective way ; the scene presented being a consulting room, with two figures, doctor and patient, and the title of the picture is " Sentence of Death." Since Mr. Collier exhibited "The Cheat " he has been sopposod to cherish an ambition to paint pictures in order to provide the public with problems, but Mr. Collier has never enter- tained any such ideas. "The Cheat" attracted more attention than any other picture of its year, not so much by its merits as a painting, but owing to the mystery that was read into it. But from the first Mr. Collier intended the story to be quite clear—that the lady standing up had turned clown a card which the lady sitting down bad marked on the back. The cause of its popularity was, in Mr. Collier's view, its own condemnation, but at the same time it gave him a reputation for painting problem pictures, of a sensa- tional tendency which he has since found it very difficult to get rid of. _ _</p>
        <p>Turkish Delight. —The making of this wholesome and delicious sweetmeat affords considerable delight to nuraeryites, and is a change from the more usual toffee. and butterscotch. Take one ounce of gelatine and k it for two hours in a gill of cold water. Place ha - pound of granulated sugar in half a gill of cold r, st and leave it to stand the same length of time, en mix the gelatine and sugar water, and boil for twenty minutes flavouring with the juice and grated rind of ona orange. The Turkish Delight should next be poured off into clean baking tins previously rinsed in cold water. When quite cold it should he cut into squares, or cubes, and tossed in a mixture of half cornflour and icing sugar.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting in connection with the Earle,- town Cricket Club was held in the Viaduct Institute, Earlestown, on Saturday. Mr. Outland presided.—The Secretary read the report and presented the balance sheet. Afterwards the officers for the ensuing year I were appointed as follows :—President, Mr. Earl ; vice- presidents, Messrs. Harwood, Smalley and Emmett ; hon. treasurer, Mr. Ormand ; bon. secretary, Mr. Alder- son ; auditors, Iklesars. H. Tarbuck and R. Robinson ; captain Ist them. Mr./P. Marsh ; vice-captain, Mr. J. Alderson ; second captain, Mr. Lemmond vice-captain, Mr. Clark ; committee, Messrs. G. OVVPII, J. Seddon, F. Roberts, G. Leigh, J. Sanders, J. Shaw, J. Smith, T. Clare, W. Harrison.—The Chairman presented Mr. Leigh with a special prise, the gift of Mr. Ormand, who promised to continue to contribute to it. Mr. Ormand was heartily thanked for his prize, and also for his excellent treasureship since the formatisn of the club. The fixture opens with Ashton-under-Lyne away on April 18th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ST. HELENS NEW ALDERMAN.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS NEW ALDERMAN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the St. Helens Town Council, on Wednesday afternoon, Mr. F. P. Dixon-Nuttall was elected alderman in place of the late Colonel Pilkington Mr. George Parr has resigned his seat, and a vacancy thus occurs in the North Eccleston Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, April 4, 1908. WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>HOSTEfISES AND lICIRODI7CTIONEI.</p>
        <p>The perfunctory manner in which introduc- tions are too often made at the present day. is never held responsible as one of the causes of that "dearth of conversation" which is periodi- 3ally the lament of hostesses, remarks "Mi- randa." At one time it was considered a duty Dri the part of the hostess to acquaint herself, as far as possible, with individual topics of interest among her guests, and to bring their sympathies in touch by a deft word when intro- ducing them; but men and women are now ,ften left, not only 'to discover each other's identity; but to exhaust their brains in• the attempt to hit upon mutual tastes or—more im- portant still—antipathies. A NEW FRENCH SKIRT.</p>
        <p>Very graceful and charming is the new French ikirt pictured in our sketch. This pretty model is typical of the very latest and most modish Kleas on the subject, and is promised enormous rpularity during the coming spring and sum- m i sum- mer. It s, as you will perceive, of the dressy</p>
        <p>order of skirt, and is suitable for wear with a dainty afternoon blouse of net, silk, or muslin. Cut to fit beautifully over the hips, it is of enormous width at the tiottom, and being quite unctiffened, and lined with very soft material, it hangs in most graceful and becoming folds. Fine, thin cloth, cashmere, and taffetas are all suitable materials for the realisation of this model. WOMEN AND 13IIRGLARS. ,</p>
        <p>4 ' ../ /7 ' 1111 / 1,/ 4'.i t II 1 / i iki , 1:,1 il 1 Ag':ii r .1 0 00,41 ',l\‘, , fr ' 'l, 1' )1 i , A /71 ', t‘ ' /1,1 j , 4 ' 1 , ~,,/ 11l // I * / 4, , .;:f.-4/V ".- f:':. q,/ I/ I -6t . ,'"\ os _-_, .t, /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>THE YELLOW PACKET</p>
        <p>BY ERNEST GLANVILLE,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Kloof Bride," "The Black Fan," "Inca's Treasure," "Commandeered Gold," "A Beautiful Rebel," "Tales from the Veldt," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>The girl annoyed me. She treated me as a harmless, indispensable attendant, and no man likes to be considered either harmless or an attendant to be summoned or dismissed at will. To other men she was gracious, winsome, coquettish, brilliant, accord- ing to their disposition and liking for those qualities, but to me she was merely offensively familiar. She would persist in walking me up and down the deck at a swinging gait at regular intervals in the day and evening on the ground that I was in need of exercise as a victim to what she was pleased to term an "abnormal appetite." And this Conduct she accounted for by the explana- tion that I was her guardian. Her guardian! This.was because some idiot, my partner Fletcher, I believe, had at the very last moment, before our ship cleared from the Empress Dock, Southampton, seen fit to place her under my charge. "I say, Maxwell," he had said, now I recollect his words, "this is Miss Rossa, a friend of mine; see that she has a good time, and oblige me." Merely on the strength of that she felt secure in regarding me as a middle-aged protector, a position which carried no privileges whatever and a few miserable responsibilities in the way of playing gooseberry. Before we reached Port Said I struck work. For two days I evaded her in the morning, slept in the afternoon, and played bridge in the even- ing, in the state-room of a grim old colonel who locked his door on the inside. On the morning of the third clay I was leaning over the rails on the shady side looking at a string of camels standing out on the skyline, when a hand was laid on my arm. "Oh! my camel, why hest thou forsaken me?" This flippancy grated on me, and I am afraid I played up to the unpleasant role allotted me by grunting. go ashore at Port Said," she went on, "and I want you to come with me. Now, don't be dis- agreeable, for I have missed you very much." Humbug, of course, but I was instantly mollified, and slewed round to see her face. She had wonderful eyes, dark as midnight, soft as velvet, with liquid depths, and her features played up to her eyes. It was a face to be looked at, and the more you looked the worse you felt. "Of course, you want me to play the humble servant." "Just we two," she murmured, with a little liquid laugh and a pressure of her fingers. True enough, when we anchored she came to me smiling through a white veil, and was beauti- fully behaved during the long afternoon, leaving everything to me, an only suggesting two things before we went on board. One was a visit to the telegraph office, as she wished ,to send a message to her uncle at Singapore, the other a call ata tobacconist to purchase a packet of Turkish cigarettes. "Don't tell anyone," she said, "but I must have my cigarette with my coffee after meals. Whenever I marry I shall smoke with my husband. It will be a tie of comradeship. You smoke a great deal, don't you? Would you be shocked if I joined you after dinner in that snug little corner behind the captain's cabin?" "Delighted!" I knew that corner. There was only.room for two. "But only on the understand- ing that you do not look upon me then as your guardian." "What must I regard you as, then ? " she asked, innocently, and then gave the cigarettes into my keeping. "They will be better in your cabin— easier to get at in case I should require a second or a third." Mine was a deck cabin, and she had taken possession of it when she gave what she called afternoon tea, because it was on the shady side, and also because that "insufferable Mrs. Bensuan " took possession of a deck cabin. "Don't forget the conditions," I called out as she sailed away with a flowing skirt to dress for dinner. "Any conditions you like, my dear camel," she replied, flippantly, yet with a glance that was not exactly flippant. It was a glance that stirred me up, for there seemed to be a certain gravity in it as if she were weighing something in her mind. The interpretation I placed upon it was naturally tinged with vanity. I believed, in fact, that she was weighing me in the balance with the object of deciding whether I would be more desirable as a husband or a guardian. I did not play bridge that night, neither did I sit with her in the corner. No, someone else sat there, a Frenchman—naval officer by his cut— and I—l—well, I ran errands for her to fetch and carry cigarettes. Twice I went to my cabin for the cigarettes, and then I struck. The girl annoyed me, and I told her plainly that I was not a servant. "Poor dear boy," she said, jumping up and dancing round me in a foolish school-girl way, "was he tired! Never mind, then, I will get the nasty cigarettes myself." And she did. They were on the top bunk, which was used as a rack, but, of course, she looked everywhere else before she thought of searching there. "You do keep your things so untidily, "she said, as she passed me on her way back to the seat. "I must really put them straight." "I prefer to have them untidy," I replied, stiffly. But the next morning after the cabin steward had put things square I'm hanged if She didn't carry out her threat; and when I expostulated she dropped her lashes and sighed. "I thought," she said, in a whisper, "you Would be pleased." Then she raised her lashes and gave inc one glance, full of meaning and beauty and electricity and brightness, "De what you like, ' I answered,with meaning; "and I will consider that you are gettting your hand in." She gave me another look, and then I left her. In the evening when she asked for her cigarettes I found that she had so completely rearranged things that I could not find them. "Never mind, ',she said, "you will talk to me about yourself." Of course, I did. With a beautiful wome,n, or five little embroidered buttons. which ,mu- who is also sympathetic, for an audience there is i late the fastening, the real fastening being down no man living who would not talk about himself w the middle of the back. On either side of this when invited to 