        <p>, The obvious moral of some recent attacks on households, declares the Queen, is that it is the duty of the State to protect its citizens from the attacks of men wbo make a profession of burg- lary. The professionals, as a rule, are all known to the police. Ninety per cent. of the burglaries now committed would be stopped if every burg- hr known to the authorities were kept safe under lock and key, as is recommended by Sir Robert Anderson, the well-known criminal in- vestigation authority. These parasites of society must be ruthlessly suppressed. It is no use pan- dering to a false sentimentality, which pities anyone but the victim, and objects to any- body being 'punished but the innocent. Time should be made in Parliament for the amend- ment of the law, which only exists for the pro- tection of the weak and the restraint of the evil- doer. It is a weak spot in our national charac- ter, a disgrace to our vaunted civilisation, that houses should be broken into in the dead of night, and for the sake of a little plunder neither their sex nor age should avail to protect women from outrages. We spend over £3,000,000 o, year on foreign missiods, to natives educated or gentle, as well as debased and ig- norant, and yet tolerate these savages at home. Until every household is immune from noc- turnal attack, and every woman and child can sleep in security without the fear of midnight marauders, the State has not fulfilled its first and most important function, the securing of the safety of the property, limbs, and lives of its subjects. IMPORTANCE OF BUTTONS.</p>
        <p>Buttons, whether because of their artistic originality or their well-nigh endless variety, play a very important part in the embellishment of the season's gowns. Quite a novelty, and with immense decorative possibilities, are the prettily-embroidered buttons edged with tiny knife-pleated frills of muslin, crepe,cle-chine,..or Bilk. Large buttons of the Empire style with jewelled centres and surrounds of gold or silver filigree are used for the adornment of waistcoats.</p>
        <p>, olives, and noticed that he did not to—uch one. Cunrrs OP RABBIT.-Cut the backs of two Cigarettes for a connoisseur at Tokio! fat young rabbits from the body, lard and braise . I looked up deep in thought, and my eyes fell half done: take up and let cool. Slice into neat on the glass opposite the window' and in it I cutlets, dip them first in beaten egg, then in I saw reflected the face of the French officer. grated bread-crumbs seasoned with grated lemon peel, mixed sweet herbs. nmshrorn ~,,,,,, o_ ~,r, van alio pepper. kry in boiling fat until a gave him time to get away without being ob d served b y me, an then stepped out The 11,-ht brown. Arrange the cutlets in a ring on a velitleman was under the shadow of the long boat. dish and fill the centre with a puree of vege- watching me I could see from the glow of hi% tables. Send to the table very hot. Serve with cigar. I yawned, and fell into a deck-chair. Hs brown gravy and currant jelly., joined me presently. . .</p>
        <p>"I must congratulate you, sare." "Thanks. Why ? " "Sacred blue! he answered, with feeling, "there is only one good fortune on the uneasy sea—so melancholy, so hot—upon which one gentleman may congratulate another so fortu- nate—the possession of a beautiful woman's regard." "You flatter me, m'sieur." "No, sacred name, no. But yes—you have put our nose out of joint." ; "Do you really think so ? " "Helas ! yes. I think it is the cigarettes. lam desolate, I have no cigarette, but you have it in your power to win her too willing kindness with gift of the smoke she prefers. Is it perhaps asking too great favour to petition you for a packet of the same—no—yes ? "Is it likely, m'sieur, that I should give away my only advantage to a young and fascinating men like yourself ? No, you must allow me to make play with the cigarettes." "I was afraid it would seem to you so. Ah, well, I will find a packet at Colombo." He bowed profoundly, and I saw by the light of the lamp as he passed that he was grinning. Late that night I carefully unfastened the yellow packet to find if there was an explanation of this too obvious anxiety on the part of my friends. It contained twelve packets of cigarettes, twenty cigarettes in each, of black tobacco, evidently French, each packet as like the others as peas in a pod. I examined ail in turn, the outside, and then each separate tebe in the packet. They were cigarettes and nothing else, and I swore under my breath at my f adish suspicions; but all the same, when I had replaced them and put them away, I took them oet again. I smoked one and it was vile so vile teat I tossed the remainder of the packet on to the top bunk and crammed the others into the The next day she was most gracious, at the ime time subdued in manner, and I often sur- pcised her casting thoughtful glances at me, jest as I imagined a girl would were she arriving at a decision. I was gratified, of course, and in a mood to do anything to please her—that is, alything, in reason. All she needed, it appears, was a cigarette. With an air I produced the packet I had s impled at midnight, and then at the sight of the Coarse Mack tobacco offered it with an apology. She was in raptures. Her glorious eyes s ,arkled, she laughed. she patted me on the cheek, and she overwhelmed me with compli- ments. They were the very, very things she had pined for, and I was everything that was good, delightful, and helpful! She loved helpful men! And so the honey flowed from her tongue until I oozed vanity. "And you will not call me guardy ' any more ? " "I will call you," she said, rising, "anything you like; but now I must go to my cabin and enjoy these." She moved away, an exquisite dream of grace, and the Frenchman cut my reflections short by moodily suggesting a cocktail. We had three, and he almost wept on my shoulder after the third, vowing that when he died there would be a cigarette marked on his heart. It was very strange, but in the afternoon, being too excited to sleep, I happened to pass his cabin and caught a whiff of smoke that smelt strongly of French tobacco. A little later I found the deck steward in my cabin waiting for me with a request that Miss Rossa would be most obliged if I would kindly send her another packet. This was extravagance. If my good favour depended on the cigarettes I must make them last. "Tell Miss Rossa I should be obliged if she •would take tea here." I set out two cigarettes from my bag, and had just turned the key when she appeared. "Is that where you keep your store, miser ? " she said, laughing. "And is this my allowance ? " She picked up the two cigarettes and ran her taper fingers along them, held them up to her daintily-shaped nostrils, and when my back was turned to attend to the teapot, in the glass I saw her slip a hairpin into the paper tube. "To-morrow," I said, cheerfully, "I will give you the entire packet." "Dear old boy!" she murmured, while a smile came to her perfect mouth and hovered about the corners all the time of her stay. But she did not smoke the cigarettes! And I did not carry out my intention of proposing marriage. But I picked up her hairpin and said I would keep it as a souvenir. She laughed, and humour or triumph filled her eyes with a brightness that was dazzling and went to my head. In fact, I had a violent headache, so acute that I could not go to dinner. Instead, I locked the door, closed the porthole, and examined every cigarette, with the hairpin as a probe. In three of the cigarettes there was a hard sub- stance, and carefully extracting these, together with the tobacco, without disturbing the rice paper envelope, I replaced the tobacco, and examined the three foreign substances I had extracted. They were paper pellets, which, unrolled, disclosed particulars of a submarine boat whose parts were on board the steamer. An element of gravity, perhaps of danger, was entering into the business. It was not tobacco that Miss Reese was seeking; but, on the other hand, the man who gave me the packet was as close as a limpet, and it would be strange indeed if she knew or even suspected that the yellow packet contained a plan. Yet the plans must have been obtained from a French source, and there was a probability that the man who had originally secured them had passed on the secret to Miss Rossa and her French naval friend. Next day I gave her the yellow packet intact, excepting for one bundle of cigarettes, explain- ing that I must keep back one for my friend in , Tokio to whom I must deliver them. Then L waited events to see whether my suspicions were justified. Arrived at Colombo my lady insisted that I and no one else was to take her ashore, for "one long glorious day of enjoyment," which, by the way, included another visit to the telegraph office. She let her spirits have full play, and I sparkled with gaiety and wit, so that even I was encouraged to unbend. It was indeed a great day, but when, on board again, I was dressing for dinner the vanity of all things hit me with the force of a flung brick when I found that the lock of my bag had been forced and that some of my things had been taken including the bundle of cigarettes and a beautiful silver-mounted revolver destined as a gift to a Japanese °Meer. I mentioned my loss at dinner, and that French- man was tremendously excited. He also had lost' something, and he was for having the steamer , detained while the police made inquiries. "All valuables," said the captain, "should be handed over to the purser." "Of course," said Miss Rossa,"everyone knows that, and as an old traveller, Mr. Maxwell, there is no excuse for you. But perhaps you have taken the precaution ? " "The purser has got all my valuables," I said, and I noticed how the lady shot a glance at the Frenchman. After dinner I lit a cigar and thought the matter over, and the more I thought the more convinced I was that the Frenchman had opened my bag. What would be the next step ? In answer to the lady's leading question I had suggested that the purser had all my valuables, and if they were determined to get those plans they would endeavour to work through the purser. How could this be done? I could see no way, and came to the conclusion that they were dished. I am not a youngster, and what annoyed me more than anything was the bitter thought that this lovely girl had been making a fool of me. It appeared that she enjoyed the amusement, for as we steamed into Singapore she came bubbling over with another suggestion for a happy day—"just we two." "Do we visit the telegraph office ? " "Not this time," she said, with a laugh. "I have concluded all my business, and this will be entirely devoted to pleasure." She ran off to dress, and I leant Over the rail. The Frenchman was leaving for the shore, and as I never throw away a chance I went for my glasses, and, finding a secluded spot, watched him. He was received by friends, to whom he talked with great emotion. Next he had a talk with a rickshaw man, aud then be and his friends disappeared. The rickshaw man re- mained, and I. took note that the rickshaw had a yellow hood. As I tin nod I saw the second officer in his cabin preparing to go ashore. I entered and closed the door. "Second, I want you to do me a service." "Let her go." "I want you to follow me and see that I come safely aboard attain. I may be wronf.,.., but I believe that an attempt will be made to get from my possession certain documents which are of importance to the Japanese navy." "Must you go ashore ? " he asked. "No."</p>
        <p>' "Then remain on deck and keep your doeu• ments safe, 'Mr. Maxwell." "My dear boy, and this lady is waiting for me to take her ashore for a pleasant outing. Besides, I may be wrong, and, any way, a felloit doesn't like to shew the white feather."</p>
        <p>"It's the Russian, I suppose. Her husband has already gone.</p>
        <p>"Her htisb'and ? " "That's right. Maids do talk, yon know." "Then it's a worse case of fooling than I thought." "I wondered what they were playing for," he said. "Better stay on board." "Is it likely—after that ?" "Good!" he said. "You go with the lady, and I'll do my part. • Anything to help the Japs!" Miss Rossa was fuming when I found her, after a longish interval. "You have been long." I explained humbly, but even then we had to wait as the second officer' with a couple of quartermasters, had taken the first boat at the gangway. She made direct for the yellow rickshaw, and as we went off I saw the second bargaining with a sturdy little Jap. Miss Rossa leant back with a sigh of relief. "Where are we going first?" "That is where you like, of course." "I have no preference. Suppose we drive right out away from the monotous presence of the sea, and get somewhere where we shall have nothing but solidity around ? " "Excellent. I suppose there are some tea- gardens, but I have forgotten which is best." "Let us put our trust in the rickshaw man. Tell him to drive to the best tea garden." _ . . _ „. .</p>
        <p>I did so,with an inward fear tha-t the "best" in this case might prove the "worst" for me, but I put my trust in the second officer, and prepared to make the most of what would probably prove my last meeting with Miss Hosea. She, however, rapidly lost her high spirits, and was more silent the further we went, until she barely answered ray questions. I lost my respect for her as rapidly. Her manner convinced me that we were driving to an appointed rendezvous for a set purpose not to my advantage. She should have taken more pains. When he stopped at last at a very lonely place, and monsieur himself advanced to help her to alight, I was moved to expression of my disgust. "You treat me," I said, "as if I were a child. You make a shocking blunder of a delicate affair."</p>
        <p>M'sieur twirled his moustaches, madame suddenly paid me the compliment of looking alarmed. "You are very droll," she said; "but sometimes I do not detect the joke." "I thought," I replied, "we were to be alone." "Oh!" she cried, and then laughed, while m'sieur muttered savagely an appeal to the "sacred blue." She took my arm and marched me into the gardens, laughing gaily. I noticed a covered palanquin, also two sturdy coolies. The palanquin, I thought, was a part of the game. What part? We sat at a table where wine was already poured out; m'sieur, managing an agreeable smile, passed a glass to me. "Your health," he said. I smelt the wine, poured it out, and helped myself from the decanter. "To m'sieur and madame," I replied, carelessly. "What is the meaning of this?" he thundered, while madame leant forward with white face.</p>
        <p>"You should know; but if you wish for an explanation I should say it means that you wished to drug me first and to have me removed in that palanquin." Madame laughed beautifully, showing her white teeth. "And why should we do this?" "I wonder," I answered, "if the reason has anything to do with the yellow packet." "You know too much, pig-head!" Then I was right. I felt pleased, and lit cigar. "And may I ask what are your intentions —what is your plan?" "Our plan is as you rightly imagined," began madame, in a voice of concentrated passion, which suddenly in the most remarkable manner broke into a gay laugh. "V'la I an addition to our happy party-welcome, monsieur." I turned and caught the stern eye of the second, backed up by two brawny tars, and a very bloodthirsty-looking Jap. Madame smiled and smiled, panting but brave; m'sieur looked savage and gloomy. "Strange," said the second, "I should have chanced upon you here in this out-of-the-wet place." "But more or less fortunate," s answered. "We have had a slight difference of opinion to our movements, but now you have come I hai no difficulty in leaving m'sieur with madame as I believe it is the intention of m'sieur to leave</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PRETTY BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our sketch is a particularly pretty little afternoon blouse, which is both smart and original in design The material in which it is achieved is the perennially fashionable and lhost useful Jap silk. Down the fiont of the blouse runs a band of fine diagonal pin tucks, the band being mitred down the middle to give the tucks a V-shape. This centre seam is adorned by four</p>
