      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>WARRINGTON TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>"Contracting Out " Condemned.</p>
        <p>Tea al'ltual meeting of the Warrington and District ildirrs' Association was held in the Wycliffe Memorial m on Saturday morning under the presidency of r. nalburn.</p>
        <p>THR REPORTS. tiZt.heFro. 13 LoNosHa.w (hon. secretary) announced 1 durio „ ership roll had increased from 117 to 155 , the g the past year. At a special meeting, called for to „PoellsrP. ose,it was decidod by a very large majority not , on'aulate with the Warrington Trades and Labour cio,t,/ja,_.l- A most successful year had been brought to a vi,"`" °3' one of their officers, Mr. Upson, being elected 14-I:4•President and ultimately president of the Lanca- 1 zhlre County Teachers' Association, which had a mem- 1 Ref over 8,000 teachers. (Applause.) 1 TREASURER (Mr. J. Upson) submitted his I,;rauts, from which it appeared that the total receipts lea.' £lOO Os. 7d. and the payments £7B 17s. 6cl. £15'1116 a reserve fund of £l5 and a balance in hand of 3 S. id. From the balance it wasdecided to give to the funds of the Warrington Children's tlipprlner Camp—a suggestion which met with hearty ' 14oval. The balance sheet, which was signed by lit,!11-1. Glover and J. Armstrong, was adopted. tile-„,' Q.W.JEFEErtv in (the secretary of the local branch of , teache Provident P 'dent SoCiety said that they started c 903 eorecWith live members and now 53 members were i llat v ed. He strongly advised all teachers who were l' 'exee",,et members to apply for particulars of this "cta"ffilY helpful branch of the N.U.T., as he was net la that the benefits which could be obtained were A el•Trally known. th'elpthis point in the proceedings Mr. Kilburn vacated pi,,,,,riesidett's chair, his term of office being now corn- Mr. ,11l Mll' and L Bowen, the vice-president, was duly wa's h— as president for the ensuing year. r.Kilburn ear"M tily thanked for his past services. The , ELECTION OF OFFICERS. . deet ,election of officers resulted as follows :—Prem- llecrLdr• T. Bowen • vice-president, Mr. J. Armstrong ; ' lipe-IaTY, Mr. F. H. Longshaw ; treasurer, Mr. J. J, Parliamentary and Press correspondent, Mr. F. taryAspa .• benevolent and orphan funds—local seore- ,,4etet' r• J. Cann ; Teachers' Provident Society—local -alr `atY, Mr. S.W. Jeffery ; secretary ladies' bureau, NA; Stansfteld•, committee, Miss Davies, Messrs. J. W. tepr erl T. D. Amos, W. H. Stansfield and G. Newborn; A,3s Essentatives on the Lancashire County Teachers' teiation, Messrs. J. Upson, W. H. Stansffeld, and F. cillgshaw. PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS. " CONTRACTING-OUT " CONDEMNED. ,130 WEN in the course of his presidential address were fortunate in having on their Education exi:ata ittee a nucleus of men who were educational and whilst they were the guiding spirits they have little fear of that friction which was all IVer44.'evalent in some towns, reaching them. That they tkvicle,la sympathy with the teachers under them was hnq "eed by the absence of the irksome regulations The restrictions associated with many committees. schoilaestion of large classes was all-important to the it h 4r3 and teachers in their schools. How was ~.,claroc)s.lible for any individual to educate and fully '4S `ab" minds of sixty, seventy or eighty pupils? It Atx Bolutely impossible • he cared not bow earnest Ileri!ifted the teachers 'night be. That the masses j1"loott'll; yet fully alive to the value of education was esR 37 the fact that wherever retrenchment was --„TY the education of future men and women was a2terep37 the first thing to suffer. The granting of '4, btiti 'lcin Certificates was another point which required N 4, • (Hear, hear.) No doubt there were at times Nyla " extreme poverty where the earnings of the r c14.11,,Nv5Te all that came between starvation and the but there were cases where children were reo:raru school at the minimum age for more -"t'he • because the parents looked upon the</p>
        <p>e°1443)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>education of their children as a necessary evil to be got rid of as soon as possible. The scarcity of pro- motion amongst class teachers was a subject which would long have a great bearing on the future of education. When an opportunity for promotion occurred in Warrington he was strongly of the opinion that men and women of long and faithful service and of proved ability should at least be given a trial. Two recent appointments led them to hope that local teachers would receive favourable consideration in the future. He proposed to deal very briefly with only one clause of the Education Bill recently introduced into the House of Commons. He referred, of course, to "contracting-out," and judging by what one read in the educational papers and the fesling locally, teachers would be almost unanimous in their condemnation of this pernicious clause. The Act of 1902 was a decided step forward towards a national system of educa- tion, and many schools which had been poorly equipped and staffed were placed on a sound footing. If that clause came into operation and managers made use of it their schools must of necessity feel a shortage of funds, and as had been the case before, the salaried of the teachers would be made to supply the deficiency to a great extent. Many of them could remember the time in Warrington when different schools were prac- tically under the control of one man, either vicar or headmaster. His will in all school affairs was all- powerful, and teachers were engagqd, dismissed, had their salaries raised, &amp;c., just as he decided. Few of them wished to see such a condition of affairs here again, and yet some things might be possible and a national system of education would be further off than ever. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>, A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Bowen for his address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>BRICK-STREET MISSION.</head>
        <p>BRICK-STREET MISSION.</p>
        <p>£2lB Raised.</p>
        <p>The bazaar to raise £2OO in connection with Brick-street Indeyendent Methodist Church was brought to a con- clusion on Saturday, and the promoters were delighted to find that the handsome sum of £2lB 18s., had been raised. The sale'was re-opened on Saturday by Mrs. James Dodds of Penketh, and the ohair was occupied by Mr. W. H. Sherburn.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said the building which they were in that afternoon was no doubt suitable for their own purposes and for the district in which it was built. He had taken it into his mind some time ago that he was opposed to bazaars but be had arrived at the conclusion that there was nothing else which could take its place., He expressed the hope that the amount they required would be realized and called upon Mrs. Dodds to declare the bazaar open. (Applause). Mrs. DODDS, who had a hearty reception, referring to bazaars, said there was a lot of work connected with a bazaar, as wall as self-sacrifice, patience and time, and time was very valuable in a district like that. (Hear, hear.) One reason why bazaars were so successful was because the workers threw all their energy and spirit into the work and she hoped their bazaar would also be successful. She then declared the sale open. Mr. J. SHAW proposed a vote of thanks to the Oleic- man and the opener. Mr. W. BARLow seconded and the motion was carried. Mr. SHERORN in responding asked the audience to support the renewed temperance oranization, who were opposing the lirewers who were doing all in their power to obtain their own selfish ends. (Hear, hear.) He hoped they would do all in their power to support the temperance cause. (Hear, hear and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
