      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>The Examiuer, Saturday, April 4„ 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ETES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ETES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS' FAREWELL. ' The Runcorn companies of the 2nd Cheshire (VII.) Regiment made a demonstration on Tuesday night, which effded the old regime of Volunteering. Officers and rank and file turned up in large numbers, and held a march past in front of the drill hall. The band played " Boys of the Old Brigade" and " Auld Lang Syne," and the bugle band played the " Lest Post." RUNCORN BRICKLAYERS' ANNIVERSARY. The members of the Runcorn branch of the Operative Bricklayer's Society celebrated the twentieth ann.i- THE CUP-TIE AT BROUGHTON. versary of their establishment by a banquet at Wilson's Warrington's hopes of retaining possession of the Hotel, and there was a large company. Mr. Walter alter Cup died at Broughton, but they died hard ! For the Lenoneuered. r n eciP. Jiel the chair, ttfiLe and various secretary o ussteo st were f second time in a cup competition the Rangers have first of all lamed a Warrington man, and then beaten the society from London delivered, an address on the union's affairs. the rest. In the Lancashire Cup Final at Wigan last season, Fish sustained a broken arm in the first few POLICEMAN ILL-USED. minutes. On Saturday, Jenkins, the crack forward, At Widnes, on Monday, John Goldrick, a powerfully- was crippled after the first quarter of an hour, and built militiaman, was sent to gaol for two months for had to retire from the game soon after the interval. having violently assaulted Police-constables Airdrie and Gardiner. Goldrick was drunk and disorderly on Satur- It is very doubtful indeed whether Broughton would day evening, and when arrested he kicked the officers, have beaten Warrington's full strength in either game. with the result that Airdrie was incapacitated. Until Jenkins went off on Saturday, Warrington looked much like beating the pride of Manchester on PLUMBER'S SUDDEN DEATH. their own ground. The Rangers had rather the worst An inquest was held at Widnes on Monday by Coroner of matters all through the opening half, but in the e body of T. Loftus Major Cross-street, Brighouse n the Loftus, Major died very suddenly on oursday last. closing stages their extra man in the scrummage told a tale, and Warrington had to knuckle under, though Loftus,' who was a plumber, was 1 engaged mending a burst on the hydro-chloride plant at Gaskell rnd they died fighting. Broughton could only cross the Deakins works, and afterwards he complained that it Warrington line once, and they had many chances in had been a very " gassy "job. Later in the evening the last twenty minutes. The score in the end was 7 he was taken ill, and died before a doctor could be points to 2. summoned. Drs. Hutchinson and Edwards, who made the post-mortem, said the dcceased's heart wrs in a I weak state, and the deceased had died from cardiac , failure. The gas had nothing whatever to do with the man's death, RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. 1 The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. G. Pilling in the chair. There were also present :—Messrs. T. Wright (vice-chairman), J. Spruce, G. Egerton-Warburton, T. Baxter, E. Britch, P. Booth, J. H. Davies, L. Bolton, T. Wright, H. Maudsley, W. Harrison, C. Horton, C. F. Bell, R. Nixon, S. Taylor, W. M. Morris, P. Collins, A. It. Norman, G. F. Ashton (clerk), Dr. J. Adams (medical officer of health), J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors). The Clerk read a letter from the Chief Inspector under the Alkali Acts, stating in reply to Mr. Grirs's (Halton) letter of complaint that the vacancy created by the retirement of Dr. Affieck in February, 1907, had not yet been filled, though he trusted negotiations were now approaching termination which would enable the Board to proceed with the appointment of a successor. in the meantime he had arranged for as many visits as possible under the circumstances to he paid in the district by the neighbouring staff inspectors. Mr. Ballant and Mr. Porter. The former was now in Ireland, but he would instruct the latter to call on Mr. Once. The Clerk said that Mr. Porter had made an appoint- ment with Mr. Once to discuss the matter on Thursday morning. The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board fixing April Ist as the appointed day from which all property acquired and all liabilities incurred by rural district councils under the Allotments Acts, will by virtue of the Act be transferred to, and vested in, the parish councils of the parish in respect of which the property was acquired, or the liabilities incurred, or where there is no parish council in the chairman of the parish meeting of the overseers of the parish. The Act came into operation on Wednesday. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Though I hardly expected Warrington to win on Saturday, I had good hopes of them doing so despite the odds, and if luck had been at all evenly distributed they would certainly not have lost. They had their chances too. On play, • Warrington should have been two tries up by nalf time. Fish seemed a certain scorer on one occasion, but after beating Hardyman he slipped on the soft ground and fell near the line. Five minutes from the interval Brooks made the run of the match, but held the ball a shade too long and a glorious chance was lost. An earlier pass would have meant a certain try for Taylor or Isherwood, but Brooks in his over-eagerness to score made an error of judgment. The teams thus crossed over as they started, on level terms with the score-sheet blank. Though Jenkins was practically a passenger,Warring- ton made a desperate attack on resuming and Taylor had the hardest of luck in not profiting by a mistake of the -home full-back's almost under the posts. Then the Rangers got clear with a penalty against Lloyd for obstruction and Claude James opened the score with a fine goal from near touch. Jenkins now hobbled off, and it was not long before the next score came. Strangely enough, it was a case of obstruction again by War- rington which cost them dearly. The penalty was misfielded by Isherwood, and from the next scrum the home backs handled smartly and Hardyman just got past Fish at the corner. Claude James made the issue practically safe with a clever dropped goal, though ' Warrington rallied again, and Fish placed a penalty shot over the bar. This was the whole of the scoring in a veiy keenly contested match.</p>
        <p>There was very little to choose btween the teams, and Broughton only won because they had the balance of the luck. They showed little skill behind until Warrington, were weakened in the scrumtnage, and then they played with more confidence. 'Warrington were decidedly superior at centre for the greater part of the game and it was a pity the halves did not open out the game more. Lloyd was a bit slow in getting the ball away and had to resort to short kicking which, while it bothered tho Rangers' defence, did not give the Warring.on three-quarter line any opportunities for combination. What samples of handling there were came spasmodically, and the Warrington backs were thus not seen to advantage as they should have been. Fish made nearly all his own play up to half-time, and was always dangerous, but in the second half he was badly neglected, and only got one pass which was the slightest use, and then he dodged half the Rangers I team before be was grassed near the line. It was a great effort, but Fish was doomed not to score on Saturday. The forwards played a great game, and Jordan did not do at all badly in Tilley's place. A couple of fly-kicks came off luckily for him, but he never missed a clear catch.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. T. Baxter in the chair. There were also present :—Messrs. J. Spruce (vice-chairman), G. Egerton-Warburton, G. Pilling, E. Britch, P. Booth, J. H, Davies, L. Bolton, T. Wright,H. Maudsley, W. Harrison E. Bretherton, C. Horton, C. F. Bell, R. Nixon, S. Taylor, W. M. Morris, P. Collins, A. R. Norman, G. F. Ashton (clerk), T. Cook, G. Pearson and W. R. Deprez (relieving officers), and J. Boyce (acting master). The Clerk read a circular from the Local Government Board with reference to the difficulty experienced by trade unions, Friendly Societies, and other similar bodies in finding accommodation for their meetings in existing baildings except in publio-houbes or other licensed premises. The circular stated that while a local authority was not empowered under the general law to provide buildings for meetings of the bodies referred to yet where the authority were in possession of a hall or offices provided for their own meetings, the authority could place the accommodation thus provided at the service of any of these bodies on terms which might enable them to have the temporary use of the premises without expense to the local authority. The Board felt that it was very undesirable that the bodies in question should be compelled to hold their meetings on licensed premises, especially when it was remembered that the business transacted at these meetings often necessitated the presence of women and children for a considerable time. The temptations and the evil results arising from this state of things were well known to local authorities and especially to Boards of Guardians, and the Local Government Board trusted that the local authority would be willing to allow the use of their buildings for the purpose of the meetings referred to, where circumstances admit of their doing so. The clerk said the matter, would not affect them because that board room would not be needed. The clerk said he had received the following resolu- tion adopted by the Guardians of the Bucklow Union with a request that the Board would pass a similar one and forward it to the County Council : "That this Board protests against the action of the County Council in retaining in its hands, money raised from the rates for the purpose of education and not raised in the year for which it was so raised instead of applying it to the relief of the education rate in the following year." Mr. Norman moved that the letter lie on the table. Mr. Spruce said he could not see what harm it would do if they supported it. The Clerk said it only had reference to the higher education rate. The money was kept in hand and yet the same rate was levied. The Chairman said he understood that the money was kept in hand to prevent the rate being raised. There was about £20,000 in hand. Mr. Norman said the matter was for the County Council to discuss and they should decide the matter. He afterwards proposed that the matter be deferred till the next meeting of the Board. Mr. Wright seconded and the motion was carried. Mr. Pilling moved that the Clerk's remuneration from the Assessment Committee be increased from £lOO to 020. Mr. Wright seconded. Mr. Morris said he had no objection to the clerk's remuneration, but they were promised twelve months ago that they would get to know what work the Assess- ment Committee did, but he had not heard anything at all.</p>
        <p>• I do not reckon to pose as a prophot, but my remarks regarding the selection of Isherwood on the ...:ght wing were fully borne out by the events of the day. As I stated in this column last Saturday, Isherwood was no better on the defence than Watkin and weaker on the attack. One could not blame him. He tried bard enough but was obviously strange and uncomfortable in a position he has not occupied for ten years. It was an error of judgment ever to have dropped Watkin,and it is a question whether the result of the match did not depend on Warringtcn's weakness on the right, for the Rangers certainly profited by it.</p>
        <p>The best man on the side of the winners was Flynn, who played a dashing game at half-back and took a lot of holding. Wilson was good and bad by ,arna, but he showed much of his old skill in the later stages of the game and paved the way for F-trdyman's try. The latter was smart on the attack, but very easily beaten by Fish and it is a gteat pity the latter did not get the ball oftener. Claude James played stylish football, and is by no moans the worst of a famous family.</p>
        <p>01 course, Warrington cannot always be expected to win the cup, but I think they would have won it for the third time but for Jenkins' injury. The semi- final draw indicates that they would have had to meet Hunslet at Wigan, and with the probability of facing Hull in the final I do not think there would have been much risk of the Cup changing hands. The Rangers have a big chance now and the trophy should, at any rate, remain in Lancashire.</p>
        <p>The beautiful weather drew a large crowd to Broughton, the number being t eturned at 21,275, and the gate money at £6OO. A half sharre of the latter should mpensate Warrington for the losses which have been experienced on several import lot matches 'his season.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the Assessment Committee's work was quite private. Mr. Morris said tl.y never heard a word about the work of the Assesdment Committee, and he did not think it was fair. Mr. Pilling said it was acknowledged by Act of Parli- ment that the Assessment Committee was a private body. Mr. Norman said that Committee had a great deal of work to do, and he did not think it wise that they should have to report to the Board. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S LEAGUE MATCH.</p>
        <p>St. Helens are due at Wartington to-day for their annual drubbing, and this will no doubt be forthcom- ing from the Cup-holders, though they have fallen somewhat from their high estate. The Saints are not the team they were in the days of Traynor, Foulkes, and Pennington, though they occasionally get the better of Warrington at Kuowsley-road.</p>
        <p>The Wai firorton team will ka: Jordan, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor, and Watkin, threequarters, Lloyd and Brooks, half backs; Thomas, Belton, Naylor, A. Boardman, P. Boardman, and Harmer for- wards. It is reported that F. G. Jenkins will not play again this season, the injury to his knee necessitating a long rest. This is unfortunate, as the Newport man would have had a good chance of selection for the Wales —England match at Easter. Shugars is also on the injured list.</p>
        <p>Warrington's League programme will conclude on Gcoci Fliday when Leeds play their postponed visit.</p>
        <p>The "A " team has its concluding Combination fixture at St. Helens, and ought to win comfortably with the following thirteen: Bramhall, full hack; Lando', Cartwright, Isherwood and Tinker, threc - quarters ; Jenkins and Daintith, half backs ; Kettle, Forst3r, Jolley, Fairhorst, Holbrook and Morley, forwards.</p>
        <p>Wiat are the odds on Warring'6on "A and Wigan " A "meriting in the final for the Lancashire Shield Competition at Broughton ?</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS Y.M.C.A. SATISFACTORY FINANCES.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the St. Helens Young 'Men's Christian ssociatihrt was held on Tuesday night, when a most encomaging report was placed before the members by Mr. W. S. Dring, general secretary. .It stated that the Association was once more free from debt, but if it was going to continue SD they would need much more support than at present. A Y.M.C.A. tent was provided for the use of the Volunteers during their encampment at LlAnberis, North Wales last August, and the committee arranged to se t the general secretary free so as to take charge of the same. The number of honorary members had increased from 47 to 77 and the total for the year was 609, against 575. Mr. H. L. Kimmis submitted the balance sheet which showed that the debit balance of £219 19s. Sd. from, last year had been cleared off, ,and there was a .balanee in hand of £4 12s. 6d. This is the first time for many years that the funds have been in this satis- factory state, and it is entirely due to the successful bazaar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY NIGHT FRACAS.</p>
        <p>John and Michael Honan, of Brook-street. and James IPManus, of Windle City, were charged at St. Helens, on Monday, withassaulting Police-constable Bradley.— The evidence was that after turning out of a public-house at closing time on Sunday night, the men went along the main street shouting, cursing, and quarrelling with other men. At the top of Brook-street Police- constable Bradley ordered them to disperse. He took one man into custody, whereupon the lionans turned upon him. John Ildnan struck him a blow in the eye, which knocked him off his feet, and they pulled the prisorer away. He then took Anfanus into custody, but the men kicked and knocked him about till he was quite exhausted. They had a desperate , struggle, and a crowd of 300 assembled, but none of I them volunteered to help them.—M`Manus said the officer struck him on the head with his staff, and he was kicked like a dog when be got to the Town Hall. His head was bleeding all night, but he was refused a doctor, although he had repeatedly asked for one.- -Inspector Lisson said than when he and other officers arrived on the scene they found Bradley bleeding and exhausted. He was unable, by reason of the brutal treatment he had been subjected to, to make any resist- ance.—Police-constable APKinnon said that M`Manus was butting and kicking Bradley when he got on the scene. Witness knocked John Honan down with his staff, and Bradley went to arrest 51‘.Nianus.—John Honan was sent to gaol for a month's hard labour, and ArManus and Michael Honan for four months each.—James Honan,another brother, was then charged with breach of the peace.—Police Constable Lock said that at ten past eleven on the Sunday night James, with his mother and another brother, came to the police office to inquire about the other members of the family. They were told, after they had made some inquiries, to go away. James then became abusive and disorderly, and caused a scene in front of the Town Hall. He was then arrested.—The Chief Corstable said this man's hands and wrists were found to be covered with blood, and it was believed that he was the man who was first arrested by Police Constable Bradley.—The Chairman (Alderman Bishop) : I think, Mr. Chief, you had better withdraw this.—The Chief Constable : I think you had better dismiss it. I can't have my officers knocked about like this.—The Chairman ; Very well ; we dismiss it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>"LAST POST" AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The sergeants of the 2nd Lancashire Royal Engineer Volunteers, the regiment which has been selected to supply the whole of the Engineers for the Western Division of the new Territorial Army, on Tuesday night held their annual dinner and concert. Sergeant-Major Hannaford. R.E., presided, and there were present Colonel Fraser (commander), Colonel Murry, Majors Say ca and Tabereer, Surgeon-Major Jackson, Captains B. Brewis, W. Oppenheira, A. C. Dobson, RE., J. H. Fox, Surgeon-Major Monks, Captain and Quarter- master Anders, Lieutenants Broom, Dixcn-Nuttall and Lomax. The proceedings were prolonged until mid- night, when " Lights out "and the" Last Post were sounded by the bngler.while the lights were turned out. When midnight was turned the "Reveille" was sounded and the lights relit, upon which the officers and aergents dispersed, amid mutual congratulations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>S FREE T ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>A waggonette running from Haydock to Earlestown on Friday knocked down a girl named Alice Grace, in Vista-road, the wheels passing over the lower part of her body. P.C. Chadwick rendered first aid, and Dr. Grey was immediately summoned and ordered hsr removal to Warrington Infirmary. BRUNSWICK MISSION CONCERT. On Saturday the members of the Brunswick Mission held a concert in aid of the mission and it was in the hands of the lady members of the chaps], who had worked very hard in getting together some of the well- known artistes of the district. It was a very succersful entertainment and each it3ra on tlae programme was well rendered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ENGINEERS' TEA PARTY.</head>
        <p>ENGINEERS' TEA PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the members of the local branch of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers held their tea party and concert. About 170 sat down to tea in the Co-operative Hall. The party afterwards assembled at the Town Hall for the concert, a capital programme being rendered. The artistes were Mrs. E. Gregson (soprano), Miss M. Knowles (contralto), Mr. J. H. Taylor (tenor), Mr. E. Knowles (bass), Mr. L. E. Horrocks (hurnorist),and Miss B.Knowles (accompanist). Addresses were given by Mr. R. 0. Jones and Mr. w.T. Kelly. The occasion was also taken advantage of to present one of their members, Mr. W. Roughley, with a framed certificate of merit, in recognition of his 40 years' service to the society. Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. for the division, spoke on Trades Unionism and monopolies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FARA FIRE NEAR EARLESTOW,N.</head>
        <p>FARA FIRE NEAR EARLESTOW,N.</p>
        <p>The farm in the occupation of Thomas Ward, Newton Common, Earlestown, was discovered on fire about</p>
        <p>eleven o'clock on Tuesday morning. The outbreak is supposed to have originated in a washhouse, a spark Igniting the thatched roof. The fire brigade was quickly on the spot, and though a gale was blowing, got the fire in hand by half-past twelve. Part of the roof was demolished, and the furniture damaged. The farmstead which is owned by Lord Newton, is insured, but not the furniture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MINER'S LONELY END.</head>
        <p>A MINER'S LONELY END.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an inquest at Warrington on Tuesday on the body of Samuel Atkinson, aged 51, a miner, who formerly lodged at 65, Station-road, Haydock. On 23rd September last Atkinson wr, admitted to the Warrington Workhouse, on order from Mr. Bowes, relieving officer of Earlestown. He came under the care ef Dr. Corker, the resident medical officer, on March Ist. He complained of pains in the back, and said he had been injured, but Dr. Corker did not see any signs of el injury. Atkinson died on Saturday from phthisis. The Coroner said the man appeared to be in a dying condition when he applied for admission to the Workhouse. The man with whom he lodged at Haydock had left the district, and no one seemed to know the deceased. A letter dated March 30th had been sent to Atkinson by someone at the Crown Inn, Skelmersdale, asking him if the officials would allow him to come out of the Workhouse hospital, and if so to go there, but it of course arrived too late.—The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NASAL CATARRH &amp; ASTHMA.</head>
        <p>NASAL CATARRH &amp; ASTHMA.</p>
        <p>THEIR COMPLETE HOME CURE. POST FREE TO SUFFERERS WHO APPLY AT ONCE. Just published, the second edition of a 43 page book fully describing Nasal Catarrh, Asthma and Chronic Bronchitis, and how these dangerous diseases may be completely cured at home. The most practical work ever issued, and should be in the hands of sufferers who desire to speedily cure themselves. lais offer stands open for 30 days from this date. A pot card giving your name and address will bring you the book frc by return post Address Dept. B. 341, Dr. Cassell's Co„ Ltd., Ayton Buildings, King at., West, Mlnchesr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LEAD POISONING AND FILE MAKING.</head>
        <p>LEAD POISONING AND FILE MAKING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man's Appeal.</p>
        <p>In the Court of Appeal od Tuesday, before the Master of the Rolls and Lords Justices Moulton and Buckley, there came on for hearing the appeal under the Work- men's Compensation Act, the applicant, a workman, appealing from an award of the deputy-county court judge, Mr. Hyslop Maxwell, at Warrington in the case of Houghton v. Peter Stubbs, Limited. Mr. Cuthbert Smith (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) on behalf of the appellant, said this was an appeal from a decision of the deputy county court judge at Warring- ton, sitting as arbitrator under the Workmen's Com- pensation Act, 1006. Houghton was the applicant, and Peter Stubbs, Limited, the respondents. They were file makers, carrying on brs;ness at Warrington and Houghton had been in their employ for thirty-five years as a file maker. In 1907 he was found to be suffering from a disease which his own doctor took to be neurosis, but later on he was certified as suffering from certain symptoms which were certified to be more or less lead poisoning. There- upon Houghton brought proceedings for arbitration under the Act, and it was from the decision of the deputy county court judge in regard to that arbitration that Houghton was appealing. The learned judge found for the respondents, holding that there was no proof that lead had been used within a period of twelve months prior to the time at which the workman was found to be suffering from lead poisoning, and he a'so held that there was no proof that lead poisoning arose from the nature of the man's employment. He sub- mitted that the learned judge was wrong in law. Mr. Smith submitted there was evidence that the learned judge misdirected himself by holding that there must be evidence to show that lead had been used within a period of twelve months prior to the symptoms of lead poisoning, and that the learned judge was wrong in this respect. _ . _</p>
        <p>The evidence of Dr. Bowden was read, which showed that he found the applicant was suffering from lead poisoning, and stated that he had known of cases of lead poisoning following on the employment of hand-filing. On the other hand, Dr. Bowden, in cross-examination. said that hand-tiling could be carried on without lead poisoning. Mr. Smith said that he was there to argue that the learned judge was wrong, because he qualified the 1 employment by a period of twelve months. Lord Justice Moulton : He was bound to do that. The onus is upon you to show that any lead wes used in the course of his employment After further discussion, and without calling upon counsel for the respondents. The Lords Justices dismissed the appeal, holding that the learned judge WPS right in saying there was no proof that lead had been reed by the employers within a period of twelve months prior to the alleged lead poisoning symptoms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Presentation to hr. J. R. Griffiths.</head>
        <p>Presentation to hr. J. R. Griffiths.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, March 25th,a representative gather-. ing of Warrington Good Templars met in Mersey- street Misson room to make a presentation to Bro. J. R. Griffiths, of Little Sutton. The present has bcsn subscribed for by the Good Templars of Wartington and district. The presentation was made in a neat. little speech by Bro. J. W. Rol 3r15, P.C.T., who eulogised the services of Bro. Griffiths during his residence here and regretted the circumstances which took him out of the district. Bro. Geiffiths su;tably responded, following up his remarks with a reference to current topics and impressing upon his hearers the necessity of supporting the Licensing Bill and doing all they can to suppress the liquor traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>BREAD IN THE BALANCE.</head>
        <p>BREAD IN THE BALANCE.</p>
        <p>When we talk of the " Staff of L se" we mean that bread is the main support of life. Bread is not all-sufficient for human rely;rements but must be constantly supplemented by other food, such es ait.</p>
        <p>Currant Bread is in itself a complete food. Sir Francis Lalting—the Mug's Physician —says : " Twenty or even thirty parts of currants to seventy parts of dough render the bread not only more palatable but much more nutritious."</p>
      </div>
