        <p>THB WARRINGTON A MID-CHESHIRE EXAMINRII • published every Saturday morning. The principal and the Oldest Weekly Paper for Warrington, Stockton Heath, Penketb, Winwick. Padgate, Lymm, Ac.. being one of the Largest and Bert Weekly Papers in Lanca- shire and Cheshire. The district covered by this Paper corn- Vises a large trading and agricultural tract in outh Lanes- shire and Mid-Cheshire. TB RUNCORN EXAMINER, published every Saturday morning. It is the best weekly paper in the Town arid District, and offers the best and cheapest facilities to Advertisers. TER WIDNES EXAMINER. published every entorday morning. Whines contains the largest nninber of Chemical works of any town in the Kingdom, and is a rapidly growing centre of industry. THEI BA.ILBSTOWN AND NEWTON EXAMINER, -L. published every Saturday morning Ilia Largest Local Paper containing the News 14 Earlestown, Newton-le- Willows, Golborne, Haydock, Lowton Ac. The district thus • Comprised Is a most important part of the Lancashire coal</p>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS EX &amp;Nur poblished every Satnrday morning ,eirculating in St. Helens. Preston Parr. Sutton. Peasiev Ot•oss, att.—the centre of thp Great Chemical and Watch Trades of the Kinfrdom. pREPAID A DVERTIsENIENTs</p>
        <p>APARTMTINTS WANTED.— HOUSES WANTED. APARTMENTS TO LET. HOUSES TO BE SOLD. ARTICLES LOST. MISCELLANEOUS WANTS ARTICLES FOUND. MISCELLANEOUS SALE One Three SIA Thirteen .1 ' Words. Week. Weeks. Week,. Weeks 18 ... Os. 61. ... Is. 3d. ... Es. Od. ... 3s. 9d. 27 ... Os. 9d. ... Is. led. ... 39. Od. ... 6s. Od. 36 ... is. Cd. ... 2s. 6d. ... 4s. Od. ... 65. od. , 45 ... is. 3d. ... 35. Id. ... 61. Od. ... 75. Od. 64 ... 19. Bd. ... 31. Id. ....Es. Od. ... 10a. Od. . THE EXAMINER. . APRIL 4th, 1908. ______</p>
        <p>The small body of London THE volunteers which marched NEW ORDER. down to Whitehall in the dead of night and planted a coffin against the pedestal of the new statue of the late DUKE OF CAMBRIDGE is entitled to more credit for deep feeling than for knowledge or common- ...se. The DUKE OF CAMBRIDGE'S enthusiasm for the volunteer force was confined within strictly moderate limits. It was the DUKE, for example, 'Who compelled the National Rifle Association to move from Wimbledon to Bisley, his reason being</p>
        <p>that he wished to develop property of his own on the edge of Wimbledon Common. But even if this fact had not been overlooked, the sham burial Proceedings would still have been farcical and absurd. Fifty volunteers who marched down from St. John's Wood to parody the saddest of all ceremonial observances have no body of opinion behind them, and their mock funeral had no correspondence with fact. Toe attitude of the Barnstaple men who welcomed the Tereitorial Scheme by a midnight parade and a telegram to</p>
        <p>Mr. HALDANE was vastly more patriotic and in ' conformity with the true volunteering spirit. The volunteer movement is not dead by any means. It is merely undergoing a change in harmony with the times and the altered condition of modern Warfare, and it is pleasant to be able to record that , in general the celebrations held by way of farewell to the old volunteer system have assumed the character of a welcome to the new Territorial Army. !, The SECRETARY FOR Wen has been at pains to explain at some length through the Press what the change actually is, and what are likely to be its effects. The old plan, as he says, was to raise units on the spur of the moment, and to think out , afterwards how they might be used. The conse- quence was the development of a system which</p>
        <p>might have led to wide confusion if the force had</p>
        <p>. even beea called upon to perform those Patri°tie duties in consideration of which it l'ithee raised. The new plan is founded on a l'°°gh grasp of the needs of a possible situation. The auxiliary forces are in future to occ uPY a particular relation to the regular army. line'? will be, as before, a second line of land dAeferice. None of the members of the Territorial 'r°l3l need under any circumstances go abroad "less they choose to do so. Their primary function is home defence, and it is only for home . defence that they can be called upon to serve. For , that purpose they will be much better organised , izuch better trained, much more effective, than the old volunteer force has ever been. They Will 'be more completely equipped than the jotold volunteer force was. They will be more .. - bumerous in the long run, and they will be brought into such rplations with the regular army lopthat they will be able, when the emergency pre aents itself,if it ever does, to act in full correspond . euce with it. We can well understand that there are many members and friends of the volunteer force who are feeling deeply touched at the dis- aPPearance of a system to which we have aeons- &amp;clad ourselves through fifty years. We have ueen proud of our volunteers, who have served 11.• their purpose well in their day. 1; ri Whether there was any real danger of invasion, Ilk.' /thee Lours NAPOLEON and his inflamed generals up' arld colonels were sending their threats across the Channel may well be, doubted. " Only the devil kla°ws what he means," said the late poet laureate at that period. But if there was any danger it was undoubtedly put an end to by the volunteer Itle'vecrient. Speaking at a dinner of the ‘• Devil's 9,,Wil " the corps of lawyers which was raised when -NAPOLEON THE FIRST WES preparing to invade ogland from Boulogne—Sir JOHN HOLKER, a former Attorney-General, said that when the threat 13f invasion was heard, the counsel learned in the law formed themselves into a body for defence,and When NAPOLEON heard of it, he said, "be didn't e°ree. But even if he had come," Sir Joini went °ll to say, "the corps was perfectly ready to march With him iLiwu to Westminster and fight an action in the Courts of Law." There was as much "erriest as jest in these observations. Threats of vasion have always died down when the people rushed to arms, as was the case early in the ttlat century and again in 1859. The new Torn. ,%l Army, when it is once in being in the form iletlaiglaed and the force calculated upon, may put tll eod not only to the possibilities, but even to teats of invasion.</p>
        <p>The House of Lords has THII NEW SOURCE devoted two days to the (IP Taxs.rioN. discussion of the taxing of is land values. The subject ra,u,"tißittremely interesting one, and it is Made rnore interesting by the assurance of the Chaucellor that there is no crazy idea of fnitins the assessment of the future on land alone. th‘tromenal amount of energy was devoted to -R,o""rashing of this dead dog in the Commons. blaiicie9in at present are assessed on land and Ise gs conjointly. They will continue to i 4saessed, but, on the other hand, t is evor that the Government intends to tap a new ree of revenue, to be discovered when the otlernment has ascertained the capital value tina:tict. People who wish to be abreast of the wL,II have now to devote a little attention to liaudexrtl'ieet• The Government has promised a ke 8 a I natio n Bill for England similar to that tosdc,l°,tiand, upon which the Lord Chancellor 111,4' "le very significant statement in the Lords. not all. These measures are but pre• Khe Scotch Bill provides that a new —4l i 5 to be added and filled up in the assess•</p>
        <p>ment rEturns. This is for the "capital land value cf lands and heritages—or the amount they would fetch hypothetically in open market, reckoned apart from buildings and structural improvements, and sold free from all burdens except building restrictions. Government, for one thing, has its eye on the enormous site values in big towns.</p>
        <p>The demolition of the BRIDVE-STREET ancient ruins at the top of IMPROVEMENT. Bridge-street has effected the most striking improve• ment in that busy thoroughfarayet witnessed. It is really remarkable what dignity and spacious- ness are given to the street by the removal of the old buildings which have :so long been an eyesore to the townspeople. It is, perhaps, unfortunate that the improvement of Bridge-street should reflect so disastrously on Horsemarket-street, the entrance to which at Market-gate now looks more like the opening of an alley or court than a main thoroughfare. The turn of the other streets will come some .day, though not all of us will live to see it. Meanwhile, the " circus " at Market-gate is in embryo. The line of the new kerb running from Bridge-street to Sankoy-strset is a section of a circle, which can only be com- pleted by the setting back of the opposite buildings —a task for future generations. We hope the authorities of to-day will push the scheme forward as rapidly as is possible and not leave the benefit of the improvement wholly to posterity.</p>
        <p>There is startling news for THE RATES.— the ratepayers of Warring- BIG INCREASE ton this week. For some PROBABLE. time there have been</p>
        <p>ominous rumours of an increase of rates but the Budget which will be presented at Tuesday evening's meeting of the Town Council will, we believe, reveal an advance even greater than the most pessimistic had ven- tured to predict. Briefly stated, the net result of the Budget is that the rates will be increased by 10d. in the £-6d. on the improvement rate and 4d. on the poor rate. The respective rates will therefore be ;—Poor rate, 4s. Bd. ; improvement rate, 3s. ; sewers rate, 2d. ; total, 7s. 10d. This figure has only been reached once previously—in 1904—and for two yesrs the demand has dropped at the rate of 4d. per annum until last year the rate was only 7s. in the 2. To deal with the causes of the increase, the requirements of the committees amount to £72,135 as against £70,972 last year, an increase of £1,163. There is also a difference in the bank balances of £6915 arising from the fact that whereas last year there was a credit balance of £2,786 on the improvement rate, this year a debit of £2,517 has to be faced, while the debit on the borough rate has risen from £1,120 to £1,490. It is estimated that the Guardians' call for the first half year will be Id. in excess of that for the corresponding period last year. On the borough rate account there will be an increased expenditure for educational purposes of £2,658, the total sum required for education being £19,329 as against £16,671. The expenditure on education thus represents nearly is. 7d. in the 2, but it must be recognized that this is the inevitable penalty to be paid for our laggardliness in this respect in the past. The only new committee for which expenditure has to be allowed is the Small Holdings and Allot- ments Committee which will rsquire £2OO. Additions to the police force are estimated to absorb £450. The sum of £3,000 which is expected to be received as a result of the litigation with the Ship Canal Company has been credited to the borough rate account. The revenue from gas works profits shows a decrease, the amount to be received from this source being £11,500 as com- pared with £14,000 last year. This is mainly accounted for by the increased price of coal. A comparatively small but growing item is the sum required for sinking funds,o entailed by loans for street improvements and other purposes. The ratepayers will anticipate with interest the detailed statement to be presented by the Chair- man of the Finance Committee (Alderman Bennett) but we are afraid it will require more than the worthy Alderman's persuasive eloquence to reconcile them to the uninviting prospect to be unfolded.</p>
        <p>The Licensing Bill still THE BeiTTLE FOR looms large in the public run BILL. view and dominates all</p>
        <p>other interests. Furiously the battle wages, but most of the sound and fury corns from the brewers' side, and it is unmistake- able that they have a bad case which is fast losing ground. The Bill grows in strength on criticism and clamour, while its author and the Government behind him remain unmoved. We see the grsat liquor interest furiously at work, spending large sums of money, bringing every kind of orgenisel pressure to bear upon the poor and the rich, in order to resist the proposal that the State shall revert after a period of years to the legal and equitable theory of the licence—the theory in virtue of which it created the monopoly in the sale of drink. We see churches threatened with the loss of subscriptions' labour theatened with loss of employment, tradesmen threatened with loss of custom, if they lend a hand to the Government which has the courage to assert public rights against trade rights. But the public is finding the brewers out. They have shouted " wolf " too persistently with the result that the Government will soon have the nation behind it in its attempt to muzzle the I Trade and curtail its power for evil. Why, even I in brewery-ridden Warrington the Tories failed to carry a resolution at an open-air meeting to the ' effect that the Bill was confiscatory. On the con- ' trary the "No's" had it by about three to one. Then, too, the conduct of the clergy has roused resentment among the more thoughtful sections of the community. Their active support of the brewers, coupled with the well-known fact that the Trade subscribes largely to the Established Church " creates the widespread belief," in a words of a correspondent, "that they are callous to the larger claims of humanity and prefer rather to side with those who batten on the swollen gains of vicious speculations, than to sympathise with the serious attempt to grapple with an unscrupulous, self-centred, self-contained, and unholy monopoly." In all probability there will not be a single Warrington clergyman found backing the Bishop of the diocese at Parr Hall on Friday evening in the splendid fight he is making solely for the noble cause of Temperance. Such a state of things is a sad refiec tion on the men whose profession it is to be shepherds of souls. They are the sheep, not the shepherds ! Fortunately, the Bishop of Liverpool can rely on whole-hearted support from hundreds of Church laymen and thousands of earnest reformers who worship under other denominations. We trust that he,and the other leaders of the fight for right, will have such a rousing reception next Friday evening as will convince the most despondent that Warrington is not absolutely and hopelessly under the heel of the drink traffic.,ll</p>
        <p>There now remains but one B/D&amp;R HAGGARD Corn p 1 et e Parliamentary AND THE BILL. week before the Easter</p>
        <p>recess. It begins with a battle-roval on the Licensing Bill. Mr. Ryder Haggard's letter to the " Times " presents the case as it appears to some licensing justices. Mr. Haggard finds that he cannot accompany the Government far. He makes, however, a useful practical proposal when he asks for earlier closing generally. As he says,men who have to rise at five in the morning can do little good at a drinking-bar six hours before. As an observant justice, he is impressed by the injury which "the cold night air " works on those gentry who come before him in the morning. On this point he is for giving the landlord a share in the responsibility. Further more, be would provide a gaol for a third offence of drunkenness, and in every prison a department for these weak beings—particularly where their language is apt to degenerate—oa the lines of an inebriates' home. He would also punish the sale of adulterated liquor. But of any millennicil outcome cif the reduction of public. house. he is not sanguine. His own district, with</p>
        <p>one exception, has more drinking-places in pro- portion to population than any other, yet, pro- tests Mr. Haggard, " we are no drunker than our neighbours." He devotes himself less to criticism of the bill than to actual recommendations for its extension and amendment. He behaves in the creation of a healthy public opinion upon the sub- ject. And so do all of us. Toe question is how best to affect the public imagination, and how to stimulate widely this enlightened moral sense.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 4, 1908.</p>
      </div>
