      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The excitement at Peckham was less a storm in a tea-cup than a breeze in a beer-glass. * . *</p>
        <p>Another captious objection to the Licensing Bill has been crushed! Dr. Dallinger states that the world will not die of thirst, as Dr. Lowel predicted, for 50,000,000 years ; and at the end of that time we don't care if the six-mile limit is increased to sixty !</p>
        <p>The banker to-day is more like a high priest than a money-lender. He assumes a portentous solemnity, he sits at the receipt of custom in a marble palace as large as a cathedral; he behaves as though he were conferring favour upon you when he accepts your money.--` Vanity Fair." * * Alderman Bennett was as active as a jack-rabbit and as grimy as a dustman while the demolition of the old shops at Market-gate was taking place. There was never a "find" but what he was on the spot, and we understand that some of the labourers did a ready trade in Georgian pennies at 3d. a time with our enthusiastic aldermanic antiquarian. * * * One of the Conservative gramaphone and gas vans occupied the space at Bridge Foot on Thursday even- ing and an open-air political meeting was held. The speakers found anything but a unanimous feeling in their favour and at the end, the resolution declaring the Licensing Bill to be " confiscatory in character" was defeated by at least three to one in an audience of about 200 people. This gives a fairer indication of the public view than a packed meeting of pecuniarily interested people such as the one in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening last week. * * * Schoolboys sometimes read their newspapers,though they may omit to read their history. Echoes of recent legislation were unmistabable in the answers givenl by two budding statesmen to the question, " What was the Five-mile Act ? " One asserted bluntly that "no man might go to a public-house unless be had walked five miles." The other ingeniously wove I together reminiscences of two controversies in this quaint statement : "The Five-mile Act was an Act that clergymen were compelled to walk five miles before they could go and teach in schools." Will Mr. McKenna accept this amendment.</p>
        <p>* * * The speeches of the principal speakers of the Con- servative party at the meeting held in Fairfield School on Tuesday evening last against the Licensing Bill, were like the " drinks " sold in ye old Tavern, fear- fully and wonderfully mixed. Certainly they were anything but at home on the subject, for on the one hand they professed friendship for Temperance and then on the other hand struck out for the sacred rights of brewers' properties and profits. As the audience held no shares in the breweries, it was no wonder that the speeches aroused no enthusiasm, for they evidently had views before their eyes of the poor rate and taxes they had to pay in support of the victims of the Trade. * * * There have been few things more remarkable in recent years than the rapid strides made in England by Esperanto, the wonderful key-language which enables people of different tongues to converse fluently with each other after a few weeks, or even days, of study. How quickly it can be acquired was proved not long ago by Professor Skeat, who knew nothing of it at 4 o'clock one afternoon, and yet, at an Esperanto meeting a few hours later, was able to read and trans- late into English a story written in the new language. Five years ago the people in Great Britain who knew Esperanto could almost be counted on the fingers of two hands—to-day they count tens of thousands and cover all classes, from our young Princes to mechanics. Church services have been conducted in it; we have had Esperanto plays, speeches, and sermons ; and now we learn that it is to be one of the subjects for the Oxford Senior Examination. * * t The brewers are getting very hard up for argu- ments. The Belvoir Hunt tragedy does not seem to have helped them much, and they are now driven to telling the workingman that he will be faced with "a six miles walk for a glass of beer on Sunday and six miles back." It is not true, of course. The man who wants a drink on Sunday can get it from the publichouse at his own street corner at the proper hours. It is only the traveller who may need publichouse accommodation at other hours, and he is no traveller either in law or anything else unless he has journeyed six miles. But what the brewers are try- ing to impress upon the workingman is that he will not get a drink at all on Sunday unless he goes six miles for it. The posters now adorning the public- house boards with this statement are, in plain English, lying posters —neither more nor less. * * * Our " neutral " contemporary published an editorial note last Saturday on the beer meeting. It was written in such a strain as to say:' "See how neutral and impartial we are." The writer commented on the large audience, and the great interest in the Bill, and informed a wondering public that opinion was divided as to which was the better speech of the two, Mr. P.igby Swift's or Mr. Harold Smith's. This was about all a "neutral " paper could, of course, be expected to say respecting such a controversial measure as the Licensing Bill. Some neutrals never even try to discriminate between right and wrong, for fear of being charged with bias. But after the diplomatic note in question, no one would think of questioning our contemporary's " neutrality " on the question of public v. brewer, even if he noticed that the most vigorous attack on the Trade yet delivered in , Warrington was barely reported. At the public meeting in support of the Bill at Thewlis-street schools on Monday Mr. Madden spoke for over an hour and said some very pungent things. The " Guardian " managed to report him in eight lines and a half. Be what you would have others to become! W. When fortune turns a fellow down the world turns its nose up.—Pulitxer. Calumny is like bad money; people who , would never think of coining it circulate it without scrap'. —Diane.</p>
        <p>The doctrine that all men are, in any sense, or have been, free and equal is an utterly baseless fiction.— ilux/ey. _</p>
        <p>Leave three men together after dinner, you may be sure that conversation will turn upon woman, and that it will be the oldest man who will begin. Dumas Fils.</p>
        <p>Deal not with things you know not. —Tennyson . A condition of affairs which gives rise to fresh trouble every day is not the right one. —Goethe . Preach what you believe to be the truth and act accordingly ; then go through life looking forward. azzini</p>
        <p>Waste no tears Upon the blotted record of lost years, But turn the leaf, and smile, oh smile, to see The fair white pages that remain for thee. —Ella Wheeler Wileox. * * * This month will see the beginning of the great- est reform in the criminal law since prisoners were allowed counsel. From April 19 every person con- victed of crime by a jury at quarter sessions, at the Old Bailey, or at the Assizes has a right to appeal on any law point that arises at his trial, and a right to appeal on the ground of misdirection or on the ground that the verdict is wrong —with the consent of the judge, or, if that is refused, the consent of the Court of Criminal Appeal, the newly created Court. , The Court consists of eight judges of the King's Bench Division, with the Chief Justice as President, and it is a singular fact that the judges who constitute the Court were all opposed to its creation. The Chief 'Justice led the opposition to it in the House of Lords, , and not only did he represent the opinion of the !Common Law judges but also that of almost every ' bench of quarter sessions in the king-lom. It is, how- ever, a well-known fact that lawyers are the most conservative people in the country, and that they oppose every legal reform, but when it has become law carry it out most loyally. The judges were equally opposed to the creation of a civil appeal °num which was introduced by the Judicature Act of 1576, but the best proof of its necdssity and of its success is that it is always in arrear.</p>
        <p>Premier in name, Premier in qnality. PREMYER CYCLES from 6 guineas.—J. S. GARLAND 51 to 55 Bridge-street Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. Science Notes. Short Story. 3. Town Council Business. Notes on Games and Athletics. Teachers' Association. Brick-street Bazaar. 6. Mr. L. G. Chiozza Money on "Dear Food." Bridge-street Improvements. Education Committee. Penketh News. Rural District Council. 7. Local Football. Board of Guardians. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Rays Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp; Colds.--Ad vt " Canada by Cinematograph." This is the subject of an illustrated lecture to be delivered in the Parr Hall, on Tuesday evening next by the Rev. J. Boyle, secretary of the Colonial and Continental Church Society. The Rev. Canon Willis will preside. The Infirmary. The Hon Treasurer begs to acknowledge, with thanks, the gift hy Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., of the sum of £7 us., being proceeds of sale of tickets for fishing in the Walton Reservoir. 'LINSEED COMPOUND of 40 years' proven efficacy for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Local Coroner's Resignation. The office of Coroner of the Honour and Fee of Halton will very shortly be vacant by the resignation of Mr. Robert Dobson, of Warrington, who has held the office since the year 1899. The appointment is in ' the gift of the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster. For Influenza Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/1i and 2/9.—Advt. Miss Felicia Sutton. Miss Felicia Sutton, of Warrington, is taking the the principal contralto part in "Caralleria Rusticana," which is being presented at the Gaiety Theatre, Man- chester, this week by the Manchester School of Music.</p>
        <p>'LINSEED CONIPOUND ' for Coughs and Colds Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall's Horses.</p>
        <p>- _ The horses, the property of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., which were used in his state carriage during the term of office of High Sheriff of Cheshire just com- pleted, were sold at Tattersall's Repository on Mon- day. The pair of brown horses realized 295 guineas and the pair of bays 193 guineas.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work at Bewsey-road. The annual sale of work in connection with Bewsey- road Wesleyan Churth will be held in the Lecture Hal on Wednesday and Thursday nest. It will be opened on Wednesday by Mr. T. Edmondson, of St. Helens, Mr. James Povey presiding ; and on Thursday by Mr. W. Bennett, of Padgate,with Dr. Bennett as chairman ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Cough and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Church Society Addresses. Last evening's address to men at the Parish Church was given by the Rev. A. J. Waldron, of Brixton, whose subject was "Christianity v. Secularism." Next Friday evening the preacher will be the Rev. Paul Bull, of Mirfield, who will speak on " Courtship and Matrimony."—ln the afternoon the Rev. Canon Cogswell, D.D., will speak at a service for women. Works Rugby Competition. The following are this week's results of the first round of the Works Rugby Competition : —Monday, Robertson's 15, Greening's 3; Tuesday, Rylands' No. 1, 11, Rylands' Wet Shop 0 ; Wednesday, Bifurcated Rivet Co. 9, Greenhalgh and Co. 3 ;, Thursday, White- cross 25, Monks Hall and Co. 3; Friday, Fletchers' 21, Thewlis' 10. For Children's Hacking Cough at night, Woods Great Peppermint Cure, Is. lid., 2s, 9d.—Advt. Warrington Chess Club. The final meeting of the Warrington Chess Club was held at Atkinson's Cafe on Thursday evening, when the club trophy, which is a silver king mounted on a massive oak pedestal, Ayas presented by Mr. E. L. Fleming to Mr. J. R. Wood, of Stockton Heath, the winner of this season's match play amongst members. Mr. Fleming also entertained the members to refresh- ments, and invited them to take part in an outing to Chester and a trip up the River Dee.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENE-FIT:-UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST</p>
        <p>BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. 1 ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street. —Advt. Tuck's Postcard Chain.</p>
        <p>The prizes in this important Competition have now been awarded by Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Soo, Ltd. Postcard chains were originated for no less than 370 of the leading hospitals in the Kingdom and Colonies, and the first prize, value £l,OOO has gone to the Royal United Hospital, Bath (3,300 postcards), while the originator of the chain, Miss M. Butterworth of Bath, gets a cheque for £5O. The 17th prize of, £25 goes to Warrington Infirmary (471 postcards), and the originator, Mr. W. Stewart Royston, Springfield, Paddington receives £6 Gs. In the competition for the most popular and artistic postcards contributed, Miss C. Walker, Fairfield-road, Latchford has been awarded the third prize, a cheque for £lO. ‘COAGULINE,"KLINK,"TENASITINE:--Cementa ofr broken and other articles.—Advt. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Lancashire and Cheshire Young Liberals.</head>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire Young Liberals.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Council of the League of Young Liberals of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation was held at Manchester Reform Club on Wednesday when Mr. C. L. Williams, of Stockport, was elected president, and Mr. T. G. Lloyd, Manchester, hon. secretary. It 'was decided to organise a campaign in support of the Government's legislation, and to co-operate along with the official organisations in forming branches in the outlying districts of Lanca- shire and Cheshire during the coming summer, and to prepare branches of the League in readiness for a general election. It was further decided to inaugurate a propaganda campaign during the summer months by means of open-air meetings, &amp;c. TRY GILLETTE SAFETY RAZORS for comfort in shaving.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. Penny Popular Concerts. There was a crowded attendance at the -last concert or the season which was held in Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Saturday evening. The committee have again been highly successful in their efforts this season and tbe concerts have been, if possible, even more enjoyable than those of previous years. An excellent entertainment was provided on Saturday I evening. Songs were given in capital style by Misses Marie Houghton, and Ethel Parr, and Messrs. Gough and Vernon. Miss Lily Locke treated the audience to I several pianoforte solos and also acted as accompanist. Mr. W. W. Whittle maintained his reputation ail a. 'humorist, and the audience were kept in high glee: The Rev. T. Miller presided and during an interval in the programme spoke strongly in favour of the ' Licensing Bin. He urged all present to support it. Not a single minister in ate Wesleyan Methodist I Conference, he said, had any interest whatever in I brewing affairs. MOUNTAIN FLAX (Lrium CATIIA.RTICIIII)PILLS An agreeable aperient. Werth a trial. 90.—Advt. .PUBLIC BIergmT.—IINDIOR THE CLOCK 10A BRST Boars In I...tarossuran.—Advt. Messrs. R. Rawson and Son.</p>
        <p>On April 6th, Messrs. R. Rowson and S ma, wine, spirit, ale and porter merchants, originally established in Horsemarket-street 90 years ago, will vacate their premises in the Market Place where their business had been conducted for the last 65 years, for new and up-to-date buildings which have been erected in Barbauld-street, Friars' Gate, opposite the Hippodrome. There is a frontags to Barbauld-street of 55 feet and a depth of 120 feet. On the ground floor are the general offices, private office, and sample room as well as very extensive warehouse accommodation for the packing and despatching of goods. A special cool store is provided for champagnes and mineral waters also one heated and ventilated for presenting ales and stouts in brilliant condition, both in cask and bottle, while in the nellars, which are very capacious, all kinds of wines are binned and stored. The stores are fire resisting, being constructed with re-inforced concrete floors, roofs, and partitions, enclosed from the warehouse by means of sliding iron doors. A. very useful travelling pulley conveys goods to the cart outside, a great deal of heavy manual labour thus being avoided. The store rooms at the top of the premises are not only extensive but well lighted and ventilated, in fact, the arrangements throughout for a future extensive business leave nothing to be desired. The buildings have been dried by the Turk "Patent process.- The architects were Messrs. W. and Segar Owen and the builder Mr. Harry Fairdough.</p>
        <p>PEELIO BMZEFTT--UNDER 'THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANC AS II inn.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>Letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P. Next week we shall publish a letter from the Borough Member on current politics. The letter (which reached us too late last night for publication in this issue) deals with the prospective Budget, Tariff Reform, Education and Licensing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Mr. Asquith and Warrington.</head>
        <p>Mr. Asquith and Warrington.</p>
        <p>Sir,—On looking up some records of bye-gone Warrington I notice that when Mr. Asquith paid his one and only visit to our town some score of years ago he was asked by someone in the hall, on rising to reply to a vote of thanks," When he was coming again?" To this Mr. Asquith replied, " I will tell you when I will promise to come again, when you celebrate the return of a Liberal member for Warrington." Well, we have a Liberal member now and the Chancellor of the Exchequer might be asked to redeem his promise of long ago. In view of the good work he has lately put in I think he would be assured of as hearty a reception by the Progressive forces as he appears to have had on his former visit,—Yours a H. F.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Cramming the Catechism.</head>
        <p>Cramming the Catechism.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your stand in connection with the Licensing Bill compels the admiration of all lovers of Temperance Reform. But whilst this matter is engaging wholesale attention there is not mach to be heard about the Education question. A friend of mine told me that his child is a pupil teacher under the local Education Committee. All vacancies" having been filled at the school where he desired his child to be engaged the Education Committee sent his child to a Church school. Here the head teacher started what were called Scrip- ture classes on one morning and two evenings weekly. My friend raised no objection to his child attending, until presently he hears his child busy memorising the Catechism of the Established Church. Being a robust Nonconformist he asked the child were such things in accordance with what the Bible contained and received a negative reply, when he said " don't learn them if you cannot believe them." He then found that what had been termed Scripture classes had developed in preparatory classes for a Confirmation service that is to be held soon, and he told his child not to attend. Now Sir, the adherents of the Established Church cry a great deal ab lilt the wishes of the parents not being considered concerning religious teaching, possibly it would be as well if they practised what they preach. The incident recited is an insidious attempt at prose- lytising which was forestalled in time. I would not like to doubt anyone's ability to teach but the following was passed on to me in good faith :—At one of the early meetings one of the pupil teachers asked what became of the souls of infants who died unbaptized and the head teacher replied that there was no difference between such a person's soul and that of a cow or a horse. And they talk about religious teaching forsooth ? Sorry to trespass on your space.—Yours CONSISTENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Dairymaids."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Dairymaids."</p>
        <p>The first visit to Warrington of " The Dairymaids" aroused much interest, which was reflected in the large audiences which have been seen at the Royal Court Theatre this week. Mr. Robert Macdonald's company has given a very pleasing and creditable performance of the popular farcical musical play. There is not a very strong narrative running through "The Dairymaids," and one is inclined to wonder why Lady Brudenell should transfer so many estimable young ladies from her model dairy at Hinstead to Miss Pyechase's Academy. But this little detail is not of much importance, so long as amusing scenes are witnessed in both Places, which happens to be the case. There is an unlimited supply of fun right through the piece, which is also well furnished with musical num- ' hers and goes with a rare swing. The three principal girls are Peggy (Miss Ernpsie Bowman) Winifred, (Miss Doris Dickens) and Helene (Miss Annie Heenan) all of whom are nieces of Lady Brudenell. The latter seems to be equally well blessed with nephews and between the two is led a somewhat lively dance. Miss Bowman is exceedingly vivacious and won warm applause from the audience by her singing and dancing, while Miss Doris Dickens proved herself the , merriest of the merry. Much of the fun is provided by Joe Mivens, A. 8., servant to Dr. O'Bvrne R.N., and in this irresistibly droll part Mr. Dan Agar is given endless opportunities of displaying humorous qualities of an uncommon order. That he makes the most of them the continuous roars ,of laughter from the audience abundantly prove. I Little behind him in point of mirth-provoking capability is Mr. Richard Telford in the part of am Brudenell. As a precocious damsel in Miss Pyechase's Academy Mr. Telford is screamingly funny. The ! scene altogether is capital, and quite a feature is made of the " Sandow " girls in their Scant drapery, I Miss Carrie Lacey leading the troupe and singing the Sandow Girl " song very prettily. Praise is due ; also to Miss Mollie Tremaine for a dignified render- ing of Lady Brudenell, and to Miss Lucy Edwin, I who is freezingly severe as Miss Pychase. A , rollicking Dr. O'Byrne is pres Anted by Mr. G-. Gordon Beg, and some very smart work is done by Miss Maud Lucy, who contributed much to the general ' fun. •</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. VISIT OF MR. AND MRS. KENDAL.</p>
        <p>A special treat will be afforded Warrington play- goers on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday evenings next when Mr. and Mrs. Kendal and their company will pay their irst visit to Warrington. The splendid reputation which Mr. and Mrs. Kendal enjoy should be sufficient to ensure crowded audiences. Moreover their repertoire is of more than usual attractiveness. On Monday evening they will present " The Elder Miss Blossom " a comedy in three acts by Ernest Hendrie and Metcalfe Wood, as played over 900 times, including two brilliant seasons at the St. James's Theatre, London; on Tuesday "Still waters run deep " a comedy in three acts, by Torn Taylor ; and on Wednesday " The Ironmastar " by A. W. Finer° (from George Ohnet's drama "Le Maitre de Forges "). Mr. and Mrs Kendal's Company includes the following well-knoun players : —Mr. Ernest Hendiie, Mr. Bassett Roe, Mr. Metcalfe Wood, Mr. W. M. Rutherfurd, Mr. Robert Burnett, Mr. F. Chas. Pool, Mr. Edmund Gurney, Mr. Thomas Holding, Mr. Wellesley Draper, Mrs. A. B. Tapping, Miss Dora Fellowes, Miss Jessica Thome, Miss Sara de Groot, Miss Gwendolen W,ren, Miss Ethel Dane, and Miss Adah Barton. On Thursday, Friday, and Saturday evenings the attraction will be a play entitled " The Mockery of 'Marriage."</p>
        <p>CONTINUOUS NEGLECT OF THE EYES is the most fruitful source of serious eye trouble. If you cannot read the small print in the newspaper ONE EYE AT A TIME 12 sway not further, JOEINSTON WATSON, EYESIGHT SPECIALIST, sbould be con- sulted at once, 84, Bridge St., Warrlustoo. Low Charges. Satisfaction Guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>EXIT VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>EXIT VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ARMY SCHEME NOW IN FORCE. The Changes Locally.</p>
        <p>At midnight on Tuesday the Volunteers as such ceased to exist and Mr. Haldane's mach discussed territorial army scheme came into force. Our readers will doubtless be interested to know how the new regime will affect the Warrington Volunteer Battalion. In the first place the battalion has ceased to be the Ist Volunteer Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment and is now known as the 4th Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment. It will form part of the West Lancashire Division Territorial Force, of which Lord Stanley is the chairman, and Colonel Edelston, who is about to resign his commission, is secretary. The West Lancashire Division was the first to get to work and on every hand there are indications of enthusiasm in the carrying out of the new arrangements. It is necessary that all Volunteers who intend to join the new force should re-attest and already quite a large number of Warring- ton Volunteers have done so. It is confidently hoped that the whole of the battalion will quickly follow suit. We believe the Warrington Volunteers were con- sidered by: the War Office to be one of the finest battalions in the whole country, ancl we have no doubt that the men will be anxious to maintain this reputa- tion of which they have every reason to be proud. They will now wear an exactly similar uniform to that of the line battalions, gild buttons being substituted for the silver ones while the officers' uniforms will be embellished with gold lace. Under the new scheme there will be no mounted infantry company but the West Lancashire Division will have a supply and transport column consisting of three companies, the headquarters being at Liverpool, Southport and Warrington It is considered no small honour that Warrington has been selected as the centre for one of these companies, which will number between 80 and 90, and thus practically the whole of the members of the mounted infantry section will have an opportunity of rejoining. The cyclists will no longer be organized in com- panies, but there will be special cyclists' battalions which will be formed in the first instance to operate on the east coast in case of invasion. rhey would be the first to meet the foe, and it will be their duty to harass them until the whole of the territorial army has mobilised. Each infantry battalion will, however, have a number of cyclists who will act as scouts, and the numb,r allotted to tho 4th Battalion will probably be 64. Nearly the whole of the cyclists' company will therefore be able to join in the battalion as cyclists, land it is particularly desired that they should do so. It is worth while bearing in mind that those who firit I present themselves for attestation will have first con- sideration in deciding who shall be allowed to continue as cyclists</p>
        <p>The battalion will consist of eight companies, as against eleven hitherto. Tbe Warrington Volunteers , may congratulate themselves that the battalion will take over the Nevton or F Corn3any of the 6t4 Battalion King's • Dverpool Regiment which may be relied upon to uphold the best traditions of the corps, for it was the finest shooting company in the 6th Battalion of the Liverpool Regiment. Those 'nen who have continued to be Volunteers up to the present time will be allowed to enlist in the territorial army for one, two, three or four years, but recruite will have to join for four years. Th, new scheme does not impose any irksome conditions. It is true that the men will undertake a little more responsibility, as they will now be able to be called up as soon as the reserve is, whereas previously that could not be done withtsat special Parliamentary sanction: No one, however, will be called to serve outside the United Kingdom unless he voluntarily undertakes to do so. The great advantage of the territorial scheme is that the Volunteers, as they have hitherto been called, will now be perfectly organised for wars, and ' eventually each division will be complete with cavalry, artillery, engineers, iafantry, &amp;to This is a distinct advance and the man who ha e hitherto been proud to serve hs country as a Volunteer will have still m?re reason to be proud of belonging to an effiaiently organized territ trial army. The 4th Battalion will this year encamp at Peel, Isle of Han, in August Bank Holiday week. Hatay I will no doubt b 3 pleased to learn that they will under the new conditions be able to go to camp for eight days and receive pay, whereas latterly they have had to serve for fifteen days before bec truing entitled to remuneration. The battalion, which on March 31st was nearly 1,000 strong, will retain its S. African battle hononrs 1903-02, which are naturally a source of inspiratioa and encouragem tnt t ) Vie inert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>TO-DAY'S Ptit.lltDE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S Ptit.lltDE.</p>
        <p>Thi3 (Saturday) afternoon will be Alio final parade of the old bittilioii. The !nen will assertible at the Drill Hill at 4.45 and march down B td-street, past A.rpley Cannons, along Bridge-street, Horseniarket street, Bewsey-road, Crosßeld-itree, and S inkey- street back to headquarters. The companies will then pr Teed independently to the Parr Hilt where they will be entertaine lat tea by Colonel Edelson, who trill afterwards explain the territorial army scheme and say fireivell to the battilion. Attestations will then be made. The F Conapiny of the oth Battalion Liverpool Regiment will take part in the Dirado, as will also the Newton Co:np my of the let V.B. Smth Lancashire Regiment, and on arriving at the station they will be met by the Band and escorted to the Drill Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>SERGEANTS' SqOKINI CDNCER.T.</head>
        <p>SERGEANTS' SqOKINI CDNCER.T.</p>
        <p>Locally the passing of the Volunteers was commemor- ated by the sergeants of the Ist V.B. Lancashire Regiment who held a hot-pot supper and smoking ' concert in the sergeants mess at the Drill Hall on Tuesday evening. The gathering was a great success. The supper was provided by Mr. John Broadhurst, the officers' mess steward, and gave every satisfaction. The chair was taken by S,Tgeant-Deu;nnaer J. W. Plinston (President of the Mess)who was supported by Quarter-Master Sergeant Wilcox (Treasurer), Colour- Sergeant F. Taylor (Secretary) tead Sorg eant-qajor Ingram. These gentlemen were ably assisted in carrying out the arrangements of the evening by a committee consisting of Sergeants Rigby, Ryder and Dagnal. The officers present were :—Major Appleton, Captains H. Cr. Roberts, Williamson, Owen and Avery, Captain and Quarter-aster Potter, Lieutenants Picton, Lapage and Woods, Second-Lieutenant Whittle and Second-Lieutenant Henshall.—After sapper the Chairman submitted the loyal toatts which were duly honoured. '-(lielour-Sergeant Spencer gave the toast of " Coloneli Elelsten aad officers of the Corps" to which C I ptai n Williamson responded.—A. letter was read from Colonel E lelsten expressing regret at his inability to be present. —Major Appleton responded to the toast of " The Territorial Army," Captain H. G. Roberts gave the toast of " The 4th Battalion, Prince of Wales's Volunteers, South Lancashire Regiment," and Captain and Quarter- Master Potter gave some interesting reminiscences of his early connection with the Warrington Cori.— During the evening an interesting musical programme was gone through by the Latchford quartette party consisting of Messrs. R. Grounds. J. Lloyd. C. E. Geldai:d and P. S nith. The huinorist was Mr. W. W. Whittle who 'was as successful as usual and was warmly applauded. Songs were well rendered by Captain Williamson, Colour-Sergeant Taylor, Mr. Geldard, Sergeant Lloyd and Mr. Gorse. Tle accom- panists were Afessrs. George Anderton and J. Gorse. —The buglers sounded the "List Post" and also `• Reveille "at 12 midnight.</p>
        <p>Just reflect when next time yon can't sleep, And you're weary of counting the sheep, i . That nsomnia chronic Yields to Ashton's Life Tonic 'Tis the cure that all suff'rers should keep. From H. M. AsTfToN, 63, Sankey-st., Warringto n.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Death of a Local Guardian..</head>
        <p>Death of a Local Guardian..</p>
        <p>Captain-Surgeon J. N. Patterson, of the Ist V.B. Prince of Wales South Lancashire Regiment, and a resident at Earlestown, died late on Thursday night after a few days' illness. Oa Monday he was carry- ing put regimental work. Heart failure was the cause of death. The deceased gentleman came to Earlestowu from Ireland 31 years ago and built up a lucrative practice. He was a member of the loca: ' Urban District Council, and only a few days ago was re-elected without opposition Ile was also an over- seer of the poor, a past chairman of the Warrington Board of Guardians,and a very prominent Freemason, he being a past provincial officer and a member of lodges in Liverpool, Wigan, Ashton-in-Mskerfield,etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION BILL PASSED BY SIX VOTES. -- ' Debate on Women's Suffrage Next Week.</p>
        <p>With its meeting on Thursday evening our local Parliament brought the first session to a close, the Education Bill being passed by a majority of six votes. It has, however, been decided to have another debate next Thursday evening, when the subject will be women's suffrage. The proceedings will be unique, in as mach as several ladies, including Hrs. Parker,will address the assembly. Great interest was taken in Thursday evening's pro- ceedings, a close division being generally anticipated, and there was a crowded attendance of members and strangers.</p>
        <p>QUE.4TION TIME. I In answer to Mr. F. A. Jenkins (Walton Division of Liverpool) the CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER gild it was his intention to levy a tax on all drinks which contained any alcohol. (Hear, hear.) The MEMBER FOR CAMBRIDGE Nr. J. N. Vernon), asked the Secretary for War if'' it was correct that guns were now being made or had been made during a recent period by contractors at about double the cost to similar guns previously made by skilled workmen at Woolwich Arsenal by machinery bought by public money—now unfortunately standing idle owing to thousands of the workmen having been dischargei by the present Government and advised by a prominent member of same to emigrate to Canada, presumably to go back to the land and fell trees. Mr. P. PEACOCK said a number of guns had been so made. The reduction referred to had only been as a result of the war in South Africa. More reductions were made by the Conservatives when in power than by the present Liberal Government. Mr. JENKINS (Walton Division) : Is it the intention of the Government to re-engage these men? Mr. PEACOCK said that if they should engage in war those men would be re-employed but he hoped there would be no war. (Hear, hear.) The SOCIALIST ME:tram FOR RUTL AND asked the Solicitor General if it was legal for Mr. McKenna to give some of the £lOO,OOO education grant for party purposes in the sense of giving sums of money to Welsh Nonconformists and evading Acts of Parliament. The PREMIER said he did not see what was meant by the question. Did the question mean that 'fr. McKenna had given public money for party purposes to Welsh Nonconformists?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>_ The QUESTIONER : I believe it is so. The PREntER : I beg to state emphatically that it is not so.</head>
        <p>_ The QUESTIONER : I believe it is so. The PREntER : I beg to state emphatically that it is not so.</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON THE EDUCATION BILL. Mr. WMASON (NeWeastle-Ondar-Lyrne) re-opened the debate on the Education Bill by congratulating the Government upon the introduction of a valuable and praiseworthy measure, drawn up in a statesmanlike way and full of democracy. He was particularly glad that the charge for education was going to be thrown on the National Exchequer and that school fees would be abolished. He wished the age of exemption from school attendance had been made still higher. It was not fair that religious tests should be imposed and he contended that it was not the teachers who were demanding them nor the scholars, but merely the parsons. Moreover there had in the past been political tests, teachers having been rejected because they were Liberals, Con- servatives or Socialists. Mr. RIGBY (Scotland Division of Liverpool), moved the rejection of the Bill. He emphasised the import- ance which Roman Cttholica attached to religious education and said they !nest have teachers who were spiritually inclined and of whose fitness they had some guarantee to give the instruct;en. If they were not granted those facilities, they would do their utmost to , upset the Bill. Secularism he contended would weaken 1 the national chantoter and it was the dear a of his co-religionists that they should continue to give the nation good honest arid straightforward citizens. (Applause). Mr. M. J. BURGESS (London University) supported the rejection of the measure, and strongly criticised the Government for having overlooked the training of character which was after all the most important. A - good religious teaching could not fail to influence character and he was of opinion that all denominations should have equal facilities to teach their own faith in the schools. (Applause). Mr. GEORGE WALRER (Home Secretary) while recog- nizing the commendable work which Roman Catholics had done at considerable sel f-secri lice in bcilding schools said the Government could not give them preferential treatment or it would defeat the object they had in viem in placing all on the same footing. Much was said about spoliation and robbery but the use of such words did not mend matters. The secular solution was the one way out of the difficulty and any other measures would,like the Bishop of St.Asaph's Bill,only meet with opposition. Mr. G. IL W. STEEL (N )rthwich) said the Nliaister for Education had evidently found himself unable to pat the Nationalist on the bask so he fell upon the neck of the Socialist and kissed him. (Laughter.) The speaker proceeded to twit the passive resisters whom he described as " 101 d. martyrs." He pointed to the great efforts which the supporters of the voluntary system had made in the past, and contendel that in return it was only just that they should have the oppor- tunity of teaching their own faith in the schools built for that purpose. He did not like the words spoliation and robbery, but he regretted to say they were only too true of the Bill. Declaiming on the need for religious teaching in the day schools in order to reach all classes of children, Mr. Steel asked : how were they to get the children in the Sunday schools ? The Rev. J. F. Mane-rEws : Go out into the high- ways and hedges and compel them to come in. (Ctieers.) Mr. E. WYNNE-Jos:Es (Secretary for Foreign Affairs) said that although the Churchmen and Nonconformists rightly claimed to have built schools, they must not forget that amongst the subscribers were many Non- conformists who ought to have a voice in the matter. He strongly supported the Bill as being the only means of establishing a real national system. (Applause.) Mr. J. NTARSII (Eceleshall Division of Sheffield) spoke in favour of retaining religious instruction and said he was surprised at the attitude of the Minister for Education inasmuch as Mr. Jeffery along with him- self attended a Warrington voluntary school where religion was taught. Councillor Batr, (Nest Wolverhampton) warmly repudiated the charge that Socialists were atheists and said the great majority of the Socialist members of that House were believers in Christianity. The secular solution, he believed, was the only one and he did not see how it could hinder the progress of Christ's Kingdom.</p>
        <p>M. BLACK (North Donegal) comdemnrsi the Bill from the Roman Catholic standpoint and de.2l)red their determination with God's help to keep the schools which they had built. The Rev. J. F. MaTrtrxws (Merthyr Tydvil) wel- comed, the secular solution, and said he wns not, ashamed to own that he was a passive resister and that he had been subjected to what the Mesnher for North- wich called " lOid. martyrdom." (Appl luse.) The Bill treated all parents alike and was scrapJlously equitable and just. Councillor NIELD (leader of the Orsoasitiou) con- tended that in bringing forward such a rt Il the o,gra- ment bad not consulted the wishes o the people and were going the right way to be rojernsd when, they epting before their constituents. a was, he believed, impos- sible to arrive at a just settlement withont compr.rniso. Mr. S. W. JEFFERY (Minister f uttion) wound up the debate with a fine speech in ,s isioh he claimed that the measure was not a Reliliens Differences Bill but a real Education Bill. Sin- of ch.. Yohmtary schools, he declared, were dear at $l, girt and WWl') not fit for children and teachers tci ap,nd so many hours in. He refuted the oft-repeated at ttosuisnt that the Ilhurch I was the first to establish an nNo zi anal system, con- tending that the Lancasterian schools were the pioneers. It was stated that the Goveu neut. had amen' ecl an amendment to the effect that na irtial exemption from I school attendance should be g-vat-d to any child. The motion that the Bill he pigclAd wts then pat. and , when the Speaker annonnced that he believed the "No's" had it. the Opposition and NAtiondiati ,yerJ wild with delight.</p>
        <p>The PREMIER, however, chtllenzed a division nnd when the votes were counted it was fount that the Bill had been carriel by 64 to sti li,vvileas to say the Government supporters were jubilint at this turninff of the tables, and there was tomultuous cheering 'for several minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>THANKING THE SPE.ARER.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE SPE.ARER.</p>
        <p>• As this was the last olcial meeting of the Session_, opportunity was taken to than't the S.,.aker (Ur. C. Plinston), whose condult in the chair has won the admiration of all sides of the f10n..0. Tributes to his impartiality, urbanity and " imperturbable serenity " were paid by the Premier, (Mr. Upon) the Leader of the Opposition (Councillor Nield), the Rev. J. F. Matthews (Socialiit). Conuaillor 'rook) (Nationalist), aed Mr. S. Wright If orlopendent). When the vote was put to the meting it was paased with loud and prolonged cheering and there was no mistaking the Bincerity of the compliment. Tho SPRAKER returned thanks in feeling terms. From a statement presented in committee, it appears that the Soeiety is in a very lionrishing condition, !having a 1,41A111C. in hand of over £lO. Mr. N. Cook, Jun.. was formally appointed deputy clerk of the Home. It was decided to hold a smokin., concert at the Lion Tintel on April 27th. 1 liefor. the House nee Mr. J. Parkinson took a iflashlight photograph of the members.</p>
      </div>
