      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. The Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) presided at the meeting of this Society on Sunday afternoon. Mr. E. Wynne-Jones gave the address, and made special reference to the Licensing Bill, and urged all present to make their influence felt by being earnest workers and supporters of everything which tended to truth, sobriety,and righteousness. Mr. Joseph Owen also took part in the meeting. Mr. Radcliffe sung " Jerusalem " and " Thou art passing hence." Mr. Payton presided at the organ.—The Chairman proposed a vote of sympathy with Mr. H. Stelfox, one of the brothers of the society who had during the week lost his wife by death. It was seconded and carried.—On the motion of Mr. Joseph 0 wen, seconded by Mr. William Bes wick,and supported by Mr. George Wallington, it was resolved: —" That this meeting heartily thanks the Government for introducing such a bold, comprehensive, and statesmanlike measure, with a view to diminishing the national degradation produced by drink, and earnestly assures the Government of its strenuous support until the Bill is passed into law, and that copies of same be sent to the Chancellor of the Exchequer, the Home Secretary, the Leader of the Opposition, the local Member and the Press."</p>
