      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>rUbLIC V. IiFEWER.</head>
        <p>rUbLIC V. IiFEWER.</p>
        <p>LICENSING BILL STRONGLY SUPPORTED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>Clergy Criticised.</head>
        <p>The Clergy Criticised.</p>
        <p>FOR THE BILL.</p>
        <p>ENTHUSIASTIC MEETING IN THEWLIS-STREET SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The local campaign in support of the Government Licensing Bill opened on Monday evening when a ward meeting promoted by the Liberal Association was held in Thewlis-street schools. The room was crowded, numbers being unable to gain admission, and the pro- ceedings were highly enthusiastic. Mr. Charles E. Parker (president of the Liberal Association) presided and was supported on the platform by the Revs. C. Harvey Cook and Sidney Pitt, Mrs. Osborne, Aldermen Burton and Pierpoint, Councillors Barton and Skelland, Messrs. W. Madden, J. Upson, J. Howes, E. J. Guest, H. W. Guest, W. H. Sherburn, P. Peacock, J. Owen, J. Elwell. W. Clegg, G. Wallington, and J. Howes, junior (Liberal agent).</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN AND THE MONOPOLY VALUE.</p>
        <p>"TRADE " ARGUMENTS REPUTED.</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN said the first point he wanted to make clear was that they were met not as teetotallers, not as fanatics, as that party was often falsely called, but as citizens. (Hear, hear.) The Liberal party did not profess to stand for teetotalism, but to stand for the cause of the people, for the general interest as opposed to monopoly interest. They contended that the liquor traffic from its character and especially from its effects' upon health, morals, and the industrial efficiency of the people required regulation. (Hear, hear.) He doubted very much if those in the trade would themselves con- tend that regulations were nanecessary.Liberals admitted very fully that reasonable facilities 'must be provided for refreshments and social intercourse. They recog- nised the fact that PUBLIC HOUSES SUPPLLED A SOCIAL WANT and that their total abolition was impracticable, unnecessary, and would be unwise. But on the other hand they contended in the words of ,the Majority Report of the Licensing Commission (which by the way received the signatures aad approval of the eight representatives of the liquor interest), they believed their statement that the number of licensed houses should be largely reduced, and agreed with them in their admission that the number of licensed houses in a great part of England and Wales is in excess of the requirements." (Applause.) The question of difference between them was in the methods by which this " large reduction" which the liquor interest through its repre- sentatives stated to be necessary, should be accom- plished. All agreed that this reduction was necessary. How was it to be accomplished ? In approaching the question they immediately came up against the question of the monopoly value of the licence.l That was the main crux of the question. Who did the monopoly value belong to. and how was it to be dealt with? In dealing with the value of anything and in deciding who ought to have it, it was well to ask themselves who created it? He thought they would all allow that the monopoly value arose from the fact that it was a monopoly; neither the brewer nor the licensed victualler made it a monopoly, but it was created by the State in the interest of the people. The great object of the trade at every licensing session was to get more licences; the more licences that were given the monopoly value of the existing ones decreased. It was the restrictions by the State which alone created and maintained this monopoly value. (Applause.) If, then they accepted the principle that thbse who made this value should possess it, the case was clear that TILE MONOPOLY VALUE BELONGED TO THE STATE and not to the trade. All licensing laws were based ; upon that fact. Even Mr. Balfour's Act of 1904 while ; it restricted the power of the State in closing public- houses by fixing the limit to the levy for compensation, that compensation was made upon the trade itself and not upon the State. If the monopoly value had been the property of the trade, Mr. Balfour and the British I Parliament had no right to tax the trade to close the, public-houses, but the compensation ought to have come from State funds. It was clear therefore that neither in this Act nor any other Act, was there the acknowledgment that the monopoly value belonged to the trade. But he could further illustrate this by an [actual fact. Lord Grey secured in the mining village of Broomhill a licence to erect premises and sell drink. I Very shortly after, before a brick was on the ground, a brewing company offered Lord Grey £lO,OOO for the I licence which he had been able to secure. It was clear from this case that the value, £lO,OOO, arose from the restrictive policy of the State through the magistrates in not allowing other people in that village to have licenses and not from any action of Lord Grey or any other licencee. It proved a further point that the value of a licence depended very little upon the question of good will or established custom. Inasmuch as there had been no house erected, there was of course no good-will to form part of the value. He thought therefore that all would admit the point that the monopoly value being ; created by the restrictive action of the State, that this monopoly value was the property of the State and not of the trade. But it was contended that the State, ' while not acknowledging by law that it ever handed over this monopoly value to the trade, tacitly acknow- ledged the trade rights to an unlimited continuation of the licences except in cases of misconduct and violation of the law, by renewing the licences from time to time.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is a matter of real moment, and of no small MR. E. J. HALL wonder that so many of the beneficed clergy of the at one of.the Conservative ward meetings put the case town, frequently give us a wrong lead. How is it? To thus "Let them remember what the action of the learn that they were present at Parr Hall, on the plat- State had been in connection with the drinking customs form, supporting a trade which is responsible for ninety of the country, that from the 15th century right up to per cent, of the crime of the country was indeed a great now the State had always tacitly consented to the surprise. The spectacle was an indecent one it not doctrine of the right of renewal, subject to good actually immoral. To win men for tho Kingdom of behaviour ; also that the State had levied probate duty Righteousness is never an easy task. It is rendered on the death of a publican, on the good-will of the ten times more dificult by the action of the Anglican business. For 300 or 400 years that had been allowed clergy. That they should support even by their to continue and grew greater and greater, and then at presence the stupid lie of "robbery and confiscation" once the Government proposed to change all that and argues on their part either woeful ignorance of the Ito introduce a doctrine of spoliation and confiscation." Licensing Bin, or complete subserviency to the brewing Now Mr. Hall, continued Mr. Parker, is a legal gentle- interest. It certainly-creates the widespread belief that I man, and ought to have studied the history of the they are callous to the larger claims of humanity and I working of the licensing laws before beginning to talk prefer rather to sLcle with those who batten I nonsense about "spoliation and confiscation " in the on the swalleti gains of vicious specu- State continuing to hold and to exercise control of lations, than to sympathise with the serious I what I have shown to be its own creation and its own attempt to grapple with an unscrupulous, self- . property. (Applause.) But let us examine more closely centred, self-contained, and unholy monopoly. Are not 1 the contention of the tacit acknowledgement of the the clergy well aware that the battle which we wage, State in regard to the right of renewal which carried , is for the rights of the people to rule themselves, over ! with it the right of possession with the profits risiee ' against an interest that seriously menaces all the therefrom. Mr. Hall must have known that when the decencies ot public and private life. The mere threat ! trade challenged the State on this very point in to withdraw support from church funds ought to have the case of Sharp v. lVakefield and the trade carried been enough to open their eyes to the nature of the the case to the highest Court, it was decided beyond alliance subsisting between the Church and the brewers any question of dispute that the right of the State to as it is regarded by the latter. Moreover, sir, do the possession of this monopoly and consequently the the clergy believe that there is property in licence ? , right to withdraw it if it was thought well, was The whole history of licensing law up to 1904 is against ! undoubtedly within its power and right and had so been the claim. The brewers have been warned repeatedly, exercised. (Applause.) I further would remind Mr. that they have no vested interest in a licence, and that I Hall that between the years 1882 and 1903 1,540 licences by those who have most right to advise them. After the were withdrawn by the magistrates on the sole ground great Over Harwell case in 1882 Mr. Thomas Nash the legal that they were unnecessary and their non-renewal had no Adviser to the Licensed Victuallers' Association in the reference to the question of mis-conduct. It is true ." Morning Advertiser " for September sth 1883, wrote that 159 of these licences were renewed on appeal to that" subject to appeal, the magistrates can refuse to ' Quarter Sessions, leaving the net number closed on renew the license of the largest, most useful, and best redundancy at 1,331. But the Justices in Quarter conducted hotel in England. I daresay that this will Sessions renewed those licences not because the local stagger many owners, but it is high time that the Trade magistrates had in any way acted illegally or unjustly fully realized their position, and did not remain an in refusing to renew, but they differed with the local instant in a state of false security." I Bench and thought that the licences were required by In spite of this the speculation in public-house the public. These figures I have not takes from a property and in licences has gone on. Who is to blame, teetotal publication but from the Brewer's almanack of the brewers or the country, when the latter steps in and 1904 with which Mr. Hall is doubtless familiar. How puts a time limit to a monopoly erected into a freehold then can it be said that the State tacitly acknowledged by Mr. Balfour in 1901—an over-genercus time limit, . the right to renewal except in cases of mis-conduct, too, in order that it may resume possession of its own? ; when in thousands of cases, the State, through the One word, more, Sir. Can the clergy seriously ' Magistrates, acted upon its right to withhold renewal believe in the cry, about the" Widow and Orphan " ? ; when in their opinion the licences were not required? Those of us who are called upon to look at the plain (Applause.) It is absurd to say that there was any tacit -facts of poverty personally wonder why the trade has acknowledgement when the State not only had the only just thought about the" widows and orphans." It legal right but freely exercised it as already stated. bas been creating widows and orphans long enough and (Applause.) I now want to refer, proceeded Mr. thoroughly enough. To quote the words of a well- Parker, to something that was said by Mr. Smith at known authority : THE MONSTRE MEETING AT THE PARR HALL "The truth is—brewery companies have been and the other night. He pointed out that during the last Ball are, passing through a .serious financial crisis. thirty years the number of convictions for drunkenness It is the nemesis which was inevitable after the wild had been reduced from 80 for every 10,000 to 60 s that speculation, inflated capitalization, and imprudent flnan- during the same period the prisoners in gaol were less cial policy of the past. It will continue until there ,by 9,000 and that the criminal convictions had decreased has been a widespread and drastic financial reconstrua- I something like 50 per cent. He said "He maintained tion, and the adoption of a sound financial policy." / that these facts ought to give any reasonable reformer This has caused the fall in the value of brewing shares, 1 the impression that the present was not a desirable time If the widows and orphans suffer, therefore, it ,is not .to introduce such a measure as the present Licensing because of the present Licensing Bill, but because those• Bill, especially when they remembered that under the who, knowing the precarious nature of licence and the / Act of 1904 licensed houses were being decreased at the real value of brewery speculation, have seriously ' rate of 1,500 to 3,000 a year without robbing anyone.' advised these people to invest their money. These are / The period chosen by Mr. Smith is a most unfortunate the responsible parties for the injuries done to the one for his own and a good deal of the argument in the " Widows and Orphans." I am persuaded, sir, that if Parr Hall. The period of thirty years covers the time enly the clergy had taken a just view of this Bill and I dating from the Acts of 1859 and 1872, which was the their own position in having the cure of souls, they real turning point from free trade to restrictions of would have been found in the company of the licences. The Acts of 1828 and 1830 practically intro- Archbishop of Canterbury, the Bishops of London, dnced free sale in beer and everyone could sell it on Liverpool, Birmingham and Hereford and a host of payment of two guineas, the idea being that by making others ; and not with the real robbers and confiscators. j beer cheap people would abandon the use of spirits and —I am, sir, very fa thfully yours, I take to the lighter beverages. This practically went on JOHN FRANCIS MATTHEWS. j till 1809 with a few small restrictions. Sidney Smith Baptist Church, Latchford. / said of the people after the introduction of this Act. April 2nd, 1908. I " Those who are not singing are sprawling, the _ _ - " The Licensio</p>
        <p>ign people . state. ng For Chronic Chest Complaints, Woods' Great Commission reported as follows ; " Never was a bill Peppermint Cure.—Advt. passed which more disastrously failed to fulfil the</p>
        <p>expectations of its quothors." With the increase of licences drunkenness increased alarmingly, crime and convictions fully doubled and it was not until 1872 that the policy of free salo was finally abandoned and the policy of restrictions took its place. The record of the last thirty years illustrates the benefit of the restrictive policy and it is somewhat strange that Mr. Smith should give himself away by quoting it. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Smith goes on to say that under the Act of 1901 licensed houses were being decreased at the rate of I 1,500 to 3,000 a year. This statement I believe to be entirely inaccurate and it is strange that Mr. Smith does not know it. The average of the three years 1905, 1906, 1907 is 887, that is the number of licences which were closed as a result of the compensation clause of the Act of 1904. But it is very doubtful if this average of 887 can continue to be maintained in view of Judge Kennedy's ruling as to the basis of valuation in the case of tied houses. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>MR. ASQUITH DEFIES THE TRADE.</head>
        <p>MR. ASQUITH DEFIES THE TRADE.</p>
        <p>The Licensing Challenge Accepted.</p>
        <p>" TOTALLY UNMOVED BY THIS CLAMOUR."</p>
        <p>"The Government is totally unmoved by this clamour. We deliberately courted it, and we are oing to face it." Such was the unflinching declaration of Mr. Asquith, the Chancellor of the Exchequer, on Tuesday night in reference to the Licensing Bill. Speaking at a banquet in London, he said these were 'fighting times, lie spoke with full assurance and some experience when he said that he had never known the Liberal party in the House of Commons or out of it in better fighting trim. They had two or three 'electoral reverses, but who of those present would not rather loso fifty seats than achieve such a vtctory as was won the other day at Peckham ? (Cheers.) "OPPOSITION'S BOLD CLAIM." Referring to the Licensing Bill, Mr. Ist-pith said the Government were ready to discuss details and to accept amendments consistent with the. governing objects and loyal to the animating spirit of legislation. (Applause). But the Opposition was not conducted on those lines. It had taken the form of a bold -uncompromising claim by a particular interest to defy the interests of the community at large. (Cheers.) The Government accepted that challenge. (Cheers.) 'They accepted it because there was no higher and no deeper principle of the Liberal faith than that particular interests must give way to the interests of the large majority. They were told the Bill was the first step of .a campaign of blunder and confiscation. He had compared the prices of the most sensitive of our securities, our gilt-edged securities, before and .since the introduction of his predatory measure. (Laughter). Take Consols, Irish Land Stock, Trans- baal Stock, or anyone of the gilt-edged securities, and 'there had been since the Bill was brought in a sensible rise. (Laughter and cheers). The Govern- ment's policy was not one of rapine and plunder. -(Cheers).'</p>
        <p>MENACE TO THE NATION." He ventured to give this warning, speaking as for -the moment the custodian of the finances of the nation, that .no greater disservice could be done to the case of property than to associate it with the per- petuation of a monopoly which had always rested on the most precarious of tenure, and which in its modern clevelpments and organisation constituted one of the greatest menaces to the well of the nation as a whole. (Cheers) The Gavernment were totally unmoved by this clamour. They deliberately courted it, and they were going to face it. They meant to persevere in the course they had taken, and they believed they had 'behind them all the influences which were making for the raising and refinement of our national life. In these .days of bread-and-butter politics it was a bracing and stimulating thing to know that they had here a case in which no man had one penny to gain, nothing but the proudest aspirations and dearest wishes Of AU those who desired to see this England of ours removed from temptations, from abuses, from avoidable causes of s offering and crime. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>The Clergy and the Bill. Sir,7—The meeting on Thursday last at Parr Hall must have been very saddening to all those who have the cause of temperance at heart ; and not the least regret- -able featcre was the countenance given to the brewing interest by the presence of a considerable number of the -clergy of the Established Church. There may be a difference of opinion as to whether the bill now before Parliament will promote temperance or not but no reasonable man can doubt that it is for this purpose alone the BM has been brought forward, and every I good clergyman will shrink from uttering a word which! would help to defeat the object which the Government I has in view, and when one thinks of the tremendous amount of misery for which the drink trade has been and still is responsible, it requires more skill than your correspondent is master of to reconcile the attendance of these gentlemen with their high profession. They attempt to justify their conduct by saying that the Bill will not promote temperance and that it also proposes: -to steal something from the drink trade. It has been said that men mostly believe what they wish to believe, and it is very probable that but for the animus which some of these gentlemen have against the ' Government on account of the questions of dis- establishment and education, they would be readier to believe that reducing the facilities is the surest way of reducing the abuse, and that withholding a licence is not stealing it,ta which case we should be spared the melancholy spectacle of clergymen using the influence of their,position in support of a trade having behind it such a record of blighted homes and ruined lives.— Yours X.Y.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>FIGHTING SPEECH BY MR. MADDEN.</head>
        <p>FIGHTING SPEECH BY MR. MADDEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. MADDEN, who had a very cordial reception, said that when the fight was to be with his hereditary and consistent enemies, he could assure the audience that it gave him very great pleasure once more to stand upon a Liberal platform—(applause)—in defence of the , grandest and most courageous Bill that had ever been ' introduced by any respoesible Minister. (Applause.) Llt was introduced as a fulfilment of the pledge of the Liberal party, for ever since the history of the Licens- ing Act and its workings had revealed to the people the destruction that it had brought upon the masses, licensing reform had been in the forefront of the Liberal programme and any Liberal Ministry would be false to .itself and to its traditions if it were not pre- pared to stand or fall by a Bill like that. (Applause.) If we do fall "said Mr. Madden " We fall with honour. (Applause:) We fall with an untarnished flag in our hands. (Applause.) We fall in the cause of justice, of cleanliness, purity, and decency in political and public life. We are not dragged at the tail of the brewers' drays. (Applause.) We are not the whipped slaves who support the Tory party in this town at the I beating of the big drum and the song of"beer."(Applause.) Their opponents, proceeded Mr. Madden, had had a big meeting in the Parr Hall and,as one of the preliminaries n beer d rink in g,they had blown the froth off. (Laughter) Having blown it off, they saw of what the commodity was made. There was a gathering of the clans there (Laughter.) Bung had come out of his corner all over England. The brewers had been driving the investors, the respectable members of the Tory party, some portion of the Church of England, but happily not all. (Applause.) This time they had failed to hold the Bishops in the hollow of their hands. (Applause.) So they would try what money could do. As Mr. F. E. Smith said a little while ago in Liverpool, they were defending the last rampart. The vengeance of the people of England was following them home and the Liberal party was ready to face the question at the polls to know whether the people of England wanted purity of life or not, and if they did not, if they were still slaves, let them wallow in their own misery. (Applause.) They would never drag the intelligence and courage of the Liberal party at their heels (Applause.) It was to be A STRUGGLE TO THE DEATH,</p>
        <p>a war the issue of which was to be whether beer was to govern this nation or this nation was to control the beer. (Applause.) Their opponents pointed gloriously to Peckham as the first-fruits of their victory. Many a campaign had been entered upon in military life when the first successes and the big victories at the beginning had not heralded victory at the end. As Mr. Lloyd George said the Peckham result was more than a passing political defeat. It was a portent, like a heavy cloud that was coming over the people filled with danger, the possible outcome of which no man could tell. They were face to face with a crisis, with what a retired Admiral said at the Parr Hall the Other night was a national catastrophy. That was a big word for the gentleman—(laughter)--but he did not venture to explain in what sense it was a national catastrophy. Though their opponents were glorying in the result of Peckham, beer was not said to have been the moving cause, and yet they knew and the Press of the country knew that it was beer which carried that election. (Applause.) While the big drum was beaten and the big brewers' band was in front, the tariff reformers were following with their little tin whistles--(langhter)— and all the other adjuncts of the Tory party were play- ing on their little kettle-thorns and triangles. (Laugh- ter.) Bat the big row was made hy the brewer. (Applause.) The "trade" were now placarding every wall with their lies about the Bill. They were making</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>STUPID AND IGNORANT APPiALS</head>
        <p>STUPID AND IGNORANT APPiALS</p>
        <p>to the ignorance of the wnkingman whom they still tried to treat as an unintelligent being. And so £lOO,OOO had gone in this propaganda and many a hundred thousand would go yet. "We will make them spend millions if necessary "said Mr. Madden. "They will have to tight this battle to the end, inch by inch to the very end. What does the Liberal party care ? It has long been in the shades of Opposition. It has been for twenty years at a spell in the desert,in the darkness of political life, without the semblance of power, but still maintaining its high principles. (Applause.) What we have done before, we can do again, and if we are defeated this time—(A Voice : We are not going to be)—do not let the brewers think for a moment that the fight is over." (Applause.) Alluding to the Parr Hall meeting, Mr. Madden said there were budding politicians from Birkenhead and other places and the local lights were dimmed—even the great Sir Gilbert Greenall who had been working terribly hard lately, driving in the wards. He had been going to the ward meetings listening to the lies that had been told by his supporters. "There is a difference between myself and Sir Gilbert "said Mr. Madden " He is a rich man and lam a poor one. He is a Greenall and, being an Irishman, I am all green—(laughter)—but not quite so green as you may take me to be." (Laughter.) Then, continued the speaker, there were a great number of parsons at the meeting. (A Voice : " Not all.") He was reminded not all. They would have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL</head>
        <p>THE BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>here in a short time—(applause)—and it would be for the credit of their cloth if on that occasion the clergy followed their Bishop.( Applause). IA was said that church building and restoration schemes and all sorts of charitable organizations in connection with churches had been forwarded by cheques from the brewers. It would also be interesting to know whether any of those clergy or their relatives had shares in the breweries or if any of them had received cheques from the brewers. lie (Mr. Madden) had not discovered yet that Sir Gilbert Greenall had given very much to the town of Warrington "" enblio building known as the ()treenail Institute or anything or Possibly because that Bill was going to be passed Sir Gilbert would tell them that he was going to do some- thing but they had spoiled it. (Laughter). The budding orator of the Conservative party, Mr. Rylands, was at the meeting. That gentleman appeared to have given up wire drawing for wire-pulling of of late. (Laughter). Mr. Rigby Swift could not really have thought of what he was saying when he said the meeting, like all the others throughout England, was an evidence of the spontaniety of the feeling of the people against the Bill. How could they call the meetings spontaneous when they were engineered in that manner by the brewers from North to South and East to West? But they could not dismay the Government that was in power. The Government had said, the Prime Minister, whom God spare to them yet for some time—(npplause)—the Chancellor of the Exchequer, Mr. Lloyd George and every responsible Minister had said that this fight was going on to the finish. (Applause.) The Government had pledged itself that the Bill should pass and was relying upon the intelligence of the people. In dealing with licences 111 r. Swift tried to draw comparisons and asked what trade would be safe if they were not to be compensated, and instanced the railways, canals and gas companies. There was not the slightest analogy. The concerns mentioned were allowed by the State to exist because they were for the benefit of the people. Who would attempt for a moment to say that the brewing interest existed for the benefit of the people ? It was well said that the poor man or man generally could do without the publican, but the publican could not do without the poor man. (Applause). He would ask Mr. Swift, even supposing there were some truth in his assertion, if he had never heard of the question of nationalizing the railways and those big institutions ? If it was important that the public should have control of the railways, canals and other institutions which were necessary to our daily life, how much more necessary was it to centrA a trade which destroyed men's homes, blighted the young faces of virtuous women and starved the little children. Why was the trade licensed? Because it COULD NOT BE TRUSTED</p>
        <p>like other tradeS'.--(ip-pi-a-us-e-.)--B-ele;:ing to the con- duct of the agitation by the trade, Mr. Madden asked if it was true that a man was thrown out of a public- house in this town last week because be refused to sign petition ? (" Shame," and a voice : " Yes it is.") Many men could tell tales if they liked, and no doubt in she course of this contest they would hear much of what had been going on. There was a principle of law that said the essence of property was that its possession was guaranteed by law. There was not and never had been such a property in a licence. (Applause.) It was i only in 1901 that t was laid down by Mr. Balfour's Act that the publican who lost the licence should be entitled to compensation. The Tories were told at that time that the Liberal party, which bad signs of coming into power, wag determined at the first opportunity to tear that part of the Act to shreds and to restore the right of the magistrates to say whether there should be</p>
        <p>a licence or not. (Applause.) He believed in his heart of hearts that many men of high principle and honour in the Tory party who worthily held the King's Corn- mission of the Peace, if they had not the courage to come out on the platform and state their opinions, would salve their consciences in the secrecy of the ballot. The publican, who was the cat's paw of the brewer, was robbed in the compensation clauses of Mr. Balfour's Act. The poor creature who depended for his life upon the breath of approbation of a Greenall , or a Walker had no voice, so far as the compensation was concerned, beyond a miserable NI per cent, and less in parts of England, over SO per cent, of the corn- pensation being swallowed by the brewers. If the publicans had the commonsense to read the Government ' Bill and understand it, they would know that under it the tenant would get his benefit. (Applause.) The brewer would not get everything into his hands. As to the -scle</p>
        <p>TIME LIMIT beli in sh,</p>
        <p>he personally did not —.owing the trade much quarter. The time limit he would give them would be very short indeed. If it came to a question of compensation, he would even be willing that the State should give them a lump sum in moderation to be rid of them at all costs as a noxious , j thing that must be strangled. With regard to local ; ' option, it was a proposition to which most men would ; agree that the majority ought to rule. He would say to the trade: "If, as you say,all England is with you, you have nothing to fear from local option at all." (Applause.) If, as the brewers said, there would be quite as much beer drunk if the Bill became law, there would be no loss to the brewers. What on earth were I these poor people afraid of ? (Applause.) The Bill, to his mind, was not strong enough. Did they not think the brewers could find sufficient money to pay , out those who wanted compensation? If they could find £lOO,OOO in a few days for the !purposes of the campaign, if they could have , such huge profits, they could find sufficient to pay out , all the publicans who had bad houses. The brewers , and the publicans said they did not want drunken men ,in their places. Why did they coma to that opinion ? Because the Liberal party and the United I ; Kingdom Alliance had been following them up for years, watching them, and because they knew , that the Licensing laws had been made so strict that if there was drunkenness in the public- houses, the licence was in danger. That was why the publican and the brewer did not want drunkenness, rake i these restrictions away, and they could all get drunk land blue in the face, as Mr. Swift said. (Laughter.) ' He (Mr. Madden) would like to get rid of the tied , house. The Bill, grand as it was, was short in the one , thing which ought to be the backbone of the measure. I The brewer must be driven back to his home. He must Ibe sent back to his brewery. He must not have two profits ; he must not be a wholesaler and a retailer. (Applause.) Publicans had been put in public-houses , under bogus agreements deliberately framed as n fraud Ito the Bench. He saw it stated that there were some 200,000 people who had invested their money in brewery ' shares in this country. What was the use of attempting , to put their claims, or even the claims of one or two I millions against the claims of the other 38,000,000 of I England's backbone and sinew ?The rights of the people , were before all things, and the right they claimed in this matter was to control the drink traffic. If they did throw a certain number of people out of employment, how many thousands would be employed by the expenditure in the proper channels of the £170,000,000 which was at present spent on drink ? He expected it would not be very long before they saw Sir Gilbert Greenall, Sir Peter Walker, Lord Burton, or any of the I other gentlemen who were at the head of brewery firms, standing at the corner of the street and begging for a copper. (Laughter.) They had all read Sir Gilbert's letter. These were the low down tricks of ! the trade that the brewers were playing at the present moment. Sir Cilbert wrote that he could not keep up his connection with the Belvoir Hunt. He (Mr. Madden) supposed Sir Gilbert and other gentlemen like him would have to smoke ninepenny cigars instead of ' shilling ones. (Laughter.) "We need not," said Mr. Madden, care about the poor people who are driven I into workhouses and gaols. We need not care about I crime that is stalking through the land ; so long as the I brewers have their race horses, their castles in the ' country, and all the riches that their nefarious trade brings them. And so the people of England are asked to shed hypocritical tears at this moment. It would be a j disgrace to any nation to listen to the singing of these men, to the crying out about their misfortunes. We will stand or fall in the cause of truth. 1 glory in the , fight. We are not afraid of them, as Mr. Lloyd-George ' said the other day when he made his appeal to the Churches. I appeal to all to come on to this platform. Let no question of nationality or religion divide us. I (Applause.) I scout the idea of a fight which is carried lon under a false and lying banner. When a man the , other day, largely interested in the brewing trade, took up that expression of an Anglican Bishop a few years ago that he would rather have a drunken and a free England than a sober and an enslaved nation, I say that is not our reading of the meaning of freedom or liberty. There is a liberty which is nothing more ,or less than slavery. There is a slavery in which men i can rejoice, and with pride can kiss and hug their chains, knowing that they have fought a good fight and have never surrendered a principle. I can walk in the face of the brewer and hold my head high and defy him, as all the men on this platform can do. We fight in the cause of truth, justice and honour. Our hands are clean. We fight for a clean programme and we want a ; clean battle and, God willing, we will put an end to the I dominion of the brewer in this land." (Loud applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>RESOLUTION. STRIKING SPEECH BY MR. UPSON.</head>
        <p>THE RESOLUTION. STRIKING SPEECH BY MR. UPSON.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN UPSON moved: "That this ward meeting expresses its hearty approval of the Government Licensing Bill and pledges itself to do all in its power to carry it into law at the earliest opportunity. He said they supported the Bill as Liberals. It embodied principles which had been in the programme of the Liberal party for years. It was a part of that great social programme which they all hoped to see carried into effect—a programme the hope of the fulfilment of which kept so many of them loyal to the flag of Liberalism. They supported the Bill as citizens. They had in the trade and in the manufacture of intoxicating drink a gigantic monopoly which exercised immense influence in our municipalities and in the State, a monopoly which was closely allied to one of the great political parties in this country, a monopoly which was exercising undue influence. Yet it was a traffic which for centuries had had to come under the control and government of the State because, although it was so splendidly orgrmized and had such immense influence, by bitter experience we had proved that the article which it manufactured and dealt in was inimical, hurtful and harmful to the best interests of the city, the citizen and the State. They supported the Bill as temperance reformers. They were told very loudly by those who opposed the Bill that it would have no temperance value, that it would not promote temperance. The vary loudness of their outcry was a confession of the fear that was in their hearts that, at any rate, if it would not promote temperance it would prevent drinking. (Hear, hear.) With regard to the charge that the poor widow and innocent investor would suffer,it was a very remarkable fact that whenever they urged any social reform touching vested interests, the poor widow was always pushed to the front. In Mr. H. G. Wells' new book, a statement was quoted to the effect that the ethical defence of slavery in America was based entirely upon the case of the poor widow who had a few slaves. At every point where they proposed to improve the moim --ift:.na of the people, continued Mr. Upson, 'where they attempte&amp; to improve the means of life, they had it thrown in tlwr face that by so doing they were touching</p>
        <p>THE INTERESTS OF SOME lELPLESS WIDOW. As a matter of fact, they were tosa that the widow was the debenture holder in most.breweries. They had taken particular care that the ordinary shares did not get into the hands of the poor widow—they paid too high an interest. The widow as a debenture holder, he took it, had an immensely strong position. She rn nut be paid the first. She was the owner of the whole con- cern and she could seize upon the stock-in-trade, the machinery and all the licensed houses for the satisfac- tion of her claim. (Hear, hear.) She was spleadidly safe, and it was therefore a mere false cry to say that her interests wolld be sacrificed. (Applause.) A speaker at the Part Hall said various allied trados would be injuriously affected if the Bill became law. That was not a new argument ; it had been urged almost from the dawn of tires. The same objection was raised against the introiiiction of machinery into works. It was said that it would displace labour. Railways were opposed in the interests of the coach builders, horse breeders, and homes makers, and any other suggestion for improvement it life was always met with the cry that labour won't( be displaced. What had been the consequence? He seed not say that all these improve- ments had led to thE employment of a far larger number of workmen than vould have obtained under the old conditions. (Appl use.) Mr. Smith, however, did not carry the argurnentfar enough. There were CERT.IN SERIOUS OMISSIONS in the list of thostwho would be thrown out of work or who would havetheir labour lessened. For instance, the Poor Law officsls would not have so much to do. The magistrates arl judges would have lighter labours. The policemen wuld have a very happy time. The lunatic asylum wrder. the prison keeper would have less and less to do .nd he said without fear of contra- diction that the hangsman's job would largely go. (Applause.) Supping tha trades which Mr. Smith referred to were aetersely affected for a short time, he did not believe foul moment that ultimately any of them would be advrsely affected but the money would go into more reprductive channels. They had heard a great deal about robery, plunder and confiscation, but there was one cies of the community which had been shamelessly robbel and ruthlessly despoiled and of whose claims thepad beard nothing. Mr. Upson gave instances of neglet of children, consequent upon the drunkenness of the parents, which had come under his notice. What, heasked, had deprived them of their necessary clothingand food? What had robbed them of all the brighsess and joy in life ? What had taken from the their . health, enfeebled their will, ruined tlir constitution, and what wa driving them out I light the battle of life with both hands tied behin them? Surely when they talked about men having 6 give up the hunting of foxes and the sailing of yaits, they should also begin to talk about the rights c.those who were dumb. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>t till g</p>
        <p>Many speakers at Parr Hall were very</p>
        <p>GLIB IN THE QUOTATION OF -SCRIPTURE. Many of the clergymen who wrote—and to their shame be it said—(applause),—for they knew the things that he knew and if they would only take the trouble to ; walk about the streets of Warrington on Saturday night they woUld see the evils about them—talked about the eighth Commandment and "doing unto others as you would be done by." There was another passage of Scripture to which he would like to call their atten- tion : " Whoso oflendeth one of these my little ones, it were better that a millstone were hanged about his neck and that he were cast into the depths of the sea." (Applause.) In conclusion Mr. Upson said : The trumpet calls to battle. If I read the signs of the times aright I am convinced that Liberals, temperance reformers, citizens will joyfully buckle on the armour to the light. We could not have chosen a better cause, for after all it is the old cause of the right against the wrong, of the weak against the strong, the cause of humanity against greed and self-interest, the cause of the dumb and suffering child against the grinding, crushing power of a hideous monopoly. I say that the lists having been fixed for the fight, with confidence we go into it and we say as the old knight said of old, , 'May God defend the right." (Loud applause) JUSTICE TO ALL. Mr. JOSEPH HOWES in seconding said he was glad , they had the opportunity to fight on a question like that. He wondered what the Tories thought at the last election when the Liberals were telling the people what they intended to do when they got into power. They might have known they would be engaged in work of that kind. The Liberal party-did not go into power to fool the time away but to do work, dud although they could , only get a majority in one House they would do what they could there and by their energy and determination blow holes through the opposition of the other. He would like to have seen the Bill stronger with reference to the closing of public-houses on polling day, but they could not get all they wanted, so they were going to get all they could and keep on asking for more. (Laughter and applause.) They were not proposing to rob anyone. They were going to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>EMANCIPATE THE PUBLICAN</head>
        <p>EMANCIPATE THE PUBLICAN</p>
        <p>from the unfair control of the brewer. The Bill con- tained the most glorious opportunity that the tenant ever had of getting some recognition when the time for the distribution of the plunder took place. Twelve months after the Bill became law some tenants would say how sorry they were that it was not passed many years before. If there was no less drinking the brewers would get as much profit as they got now, and if there was less drinking there would be more happy homes. (Applause). The brewers imagined that they had this town in the hollow of their hands. Warrington had a peculiar reputation all through the country for a great many years. It had been said to him on many occasions thPt many things could be done successfully in other towns but not in Warrington. They knew what they accomplished at the last election—(applause)—and they would open the eyes of the brewers in this agitation. If the people wanted drink of any sort,why could they not buy their drink in a free house, and why would not the publican allow customers to buy anybody's drink they referred ? They would emancipate the publican from the control of the brewer, and emancipate the people who wanted emancipating from the control of both. They would do justice to all. They proceeded on common sense lines, and although the Bill did not satisfy 411 the total abstainers, it was a great blow in the right direction. (Applause.) The resolution was carried with one dissentient. CHAIRMAN THANKED.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. EIAECVEY Coox moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. WALLINGTON, in seconding, said the Bill was the best ever introduced in Parliament within his recollection and if they did not support it, no Govern- ment would take up the matter for years to come. The vote having been heartily accorded, the meeting closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>MEETING AT PENKETH. "Under Which King."</head>
        <p>MEETING AT PENKETH. "Under Which King."</p>
        <p>A public meeting in support of the Licensing Bill was held in Penketh Wesleyan School on Wednesday evening. There was a large attendance and the opinions expressed by the speakers met with enthusiastic support. Councillor Stott presided and was supported on the platform by Mr. Gilbert J. Tickle (Liverpool), Mr. C. E. Parker, and Mr. W. H. Sherburia. The CHAIRMAN said they were met to support the ' Government in their courageous action in dealing with the licensing question. They as temperance reformers had been slow in getting into stride but although they might have some little leeway to make up, he believed that in the end they would be successful. (Applause.) The present campaign was one of the most remark- able he had ever known. The Bill was having support from Liberals, Conservatives, Nationalists, Labour men and Socialists, and he did not remember any other question upon which all those points of view had been concentrated. Nothing had plessed him more than the attitude of the Labour members and not least, of the member for that constituency. (Applause.) It was hinted to him (Councillor Stott) some time ago that there was some little suspiciou that Mr. Seddon would not be quite sound on licensing reform, but they found that in a speech on Saturday Mr. Seddon declared that even if it meant his defeat at the next election by 5,401 votes he wou'xi not in the least withdraw his support from the Licensing Bill. (Applause). The campaign in opposi- tion to the Bill was one of the most selfish and immoral he could remember. So far as the brewers were con- cerned, the question was purely one of profits and interests. It was rather surprising that these millionaire brewers should be looking after the interests of the poor widows and orphans, but it must be remembered that these men had invited trustees and widows to invest in breweries and now when temperance men , were trying to get back to the public the monopoly j value which should always have belonged to it, the brewers were trying to ride on the backs of the poor I working men, widows and orphans to retain the power which they had used very often unscrupulously during the last few years. As to the immorality of the campaign, the brewers were threatening that not only would churches but all charitable institutions suffer if the Bill become law. If the Church was to rest its foundations on the large brewing interets,the sooner it was disestablished or done away with the better. (Applause.) Hitherto they had been led to believe that all sections of the Christian movement were in favour of temperance reform but those who read the reports of the meeting held in Warrington last week were some- what shocked to find that a large number of the War- rington clergy were on the platform with millionaire brewers and their friends in opposition to the Bill. (" Shame.") He was, however, glad to flod that the Warrington clergy were not typical of the Church of , England clergy throughout the country. Such men as Bishop Gore and many other bishops bad sufficient courage to stand by and support the principles which they had been avowing during the last ten or twenty years. He (Councillor Stott) did not agree with all the details of the Bill, but they were not met to dis- C 11,99 the points on which they disagreed but to support the main principles of the measure. (Applause.) Mr. 0. J. TICKLE then addressed the meeting. The Bill, he said, was not an extreme temperance measure. If it were, it would be an entirely different Bill from the one they were discussing. If Mr. Leif Jones had brought in the Bill on behalf of the United Kingdom Alliance, there would have been no time limit, and the ,1 Bill would have included total and immediate Sunday closing, and a full measure of local veto on all licences, Many of them felt that in supporting the Government measure they had gone their part of the compromise, and they had given almost as much as ought I to be given in the direction of moderation. The Bill, they believed, would effect a considerablb reduction in drinking facilities and would very largely promote the cause of temperance. (Applause.) The method eronosed was not an immarlinto extinction of ' the liquor traffic, but a gradual diminution of the temptations which were in our midst. The statements I in regard to the displacement of labour had been ! grossly exaggerated, for it would be very gradual and I would hardly be felt by the country at all. For one man thrown out of employment through sobriety, employ- ment would he given for three or four more in toe legitimate trades of the country. Money transferred from the liquor traffic to the legitimate trades would increase employment in a far greater ratio than it i destroyed it n the liquor trade. As to the accusations of robbery and spoliation, Mr. Sloan, M.P., put the question in a nutshell when be said it was a case of the thief being found " out. The brewers were eloquent about the widow and the poor investor, but whenever they talked about robbery and confiscation they ought to look into their own homes to see what spoil they had there. Mr. Tickle referred to some of the brewery flotations which had taken place and characterised them as "wild cat investments." He pointed out that many of the investors knew the law and it was because of the big dividends that they were willing to take the risk. Men and women who put their money into that which was demoralizing and degrading the community for the sake of dividends were bad citizens and they did not deserve consideration when trouble come. If they put their money in any other business and hard times came, they would be laughed at if they asked the State to protect them. The public had no right to be called upon to back the gambling debts of speculators. (Applause.) While a licence appre- ciated the value of a house it destroyed the value of the surruunding property to almost the same extent. While the brewers had been piling up their money they had been crushing down their neighbours who were unfortunate enough to be near the public- houses. It was said that the Bill would do nothing to reduce drunkenness. If so, why did the brewers make all this fuss about it ? He was quite sure, however, that it would do something to make the country more sober. There was no more striking instance of the power of reduction and restriction than the city of Liverpool, where in the old free licence daps the arrests for drunkenness got up to within 50 of 25,000 out of a population of 500,000, whereas in 1903 the arrests were only 5,000 with a population of 650,000. Reduction and restriction had made Liverpool one of the cleanest cities in the United Kingdom, (Applause.) The same applied to Bootle where in fourteen years the convictions for drunkenness came down from 1405 to 394, and the population was one half as large again. In conclusion Mr. Tickle asked his hearers</p>
        <p>not to run away with the idea that they bad won the i victory. He believed they were going to win— (applause)—but only on the one condition that they ; worked and prayed. Never in his experience had such ; , pressure been brought to bear on those who were sup- I • porting a Bill. "We have to stand behind the people , who are threatened " said Mr. Tickle "and support , them by every means in our power. If unfortunately ; pressure comes into our own lives and businesses, let us ; seek to be true. It is a question of under which King ; are we going to serve? is Burton or Britain to rule? Who is to be King of the conscience: Bacchus or ; Christ ?" (Applause). ; Mr. W. H. SITERBURN moved: "That this public • meeting of the inhabitants of Penketh and District heartily thanks the Government for the courageous and statesmanlike Bill which has been introduced into the • House of Commons. It pledges itself to give to the ' Bill its hearty approval and to do all in its power to aid ;in carrying it into law." He contended that the Bill, instead of being a blow at the freedom of the working- man, was a blow for his freedom. (Applause.) There 1 ware immense trusts behind the agitation against the ; Bill, and when they realized the hold these companies ' had on the trade it was easy to explain the panic. Mr. Sherburn referred to the low rating of public-houses as compared with the valuations on which compensation Iwas paid, and said municipalities had been robbed , awfully in that way. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER seconded and emphasised the fact that they wanted the co-operation of the moderate • drinker in restricting the abuses which arose from I drunkenness and excess. The Bill conformed to the I reasonable wants of the people in regard to obtaining intoxicating drinks. Many parts of the country were grossly over-licensed and the Bill provided that, spread • over a period of fourteen years, the exGessive licences I should be withheld. The people had a right to control a trade which imposed such burdens on the community. , It was not so much a question of the licensed victualler ; which arose in dealing with the traffic. The real point of combat was the gigantic brewing companies, for I only one in ten of the public-houses was a free house. They wanted publicans on their side in the matter, because the Bill dealt in a just manner with the man who lost his livelihood. The great question which should move them all in supporting the Bill was the need that there was of standing by those who were • ; weak and especially of protecting the little children , who were the greatest sufferers. The Bill was honest, reasonable and moderate and ought to appeal to every I moderate drinker in the land who loved his country • , and his kind. (Applause.) I The resolution was carried unanimously. Mr. J. G. WITRINSUAW moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman and speakers. He pointed out that the people who were now crying out about being robbed had for years been robbing the citizens by not bearing their just share of the rates. (Applause). ' Mr. FAULKNER seconded and said the crusade for the Bill had united the best of all sections of the community. Mr. A. SEED supported. • The Rev. J. GUN:CELL, in putting the resolution, said ' much had been made of the financial side of the measure, but there were great humanitarian aspects which were worthy of grave consideration. The Country would bear a very different aspect fifty years hence, and less than that, if the Bill passed and was ' wisely administered, as he believed it would be. ; (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded. Mr. JAMES DODDS, who arrived towards the close of the meeting, having been detained at a meeting of the I Executive of the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League, was invited to say a few words. He said there was no doubt they would have to fight the issue not on ; the question of the length of the time limit, but of • whether there was to be a time limit at all. So far there had been little opposition to the conferring of local option which was one of the reforms that temper- ance workers had been fighting for during the past forty or fifty years. He expressed the pleasure which it afforded him to find that Penketh was right on that question, and he hoped they would always, as in the Ipast, be in the front on these great moral questions. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>CITIZENS' TEMPERANCE LEAGUE. -- – Meeting in Latchford Ward.</head>
        <p>CITIZENS' TEMPERANCE LEAGUE. -- - Meeting in Latchford Ward.</p>
        <p>The first ward meeting promoted by. the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League was held m the Emmanuel Church Institute, Knutsford-road on Thursday evening. Alderman Pierpoint presided over a fair attendance and was supported by the Revs. J. F. Matthews and C. Harvey Cook, Mr. E. J. Guest and Mr. John Maginn. The CHAIRMAN pointed out that he was not a teetotaller and he was a Churchman, and perhaps some people might say he was out of place there, because the majority of the Church of England ministers in War- rington seemed to have ranged themselves on the side of the brewers in that agitation. (" Shame.") Perhaps it did appear somewhat singular that be as a member of the Church of England should be in opposition to rev. gentlemen belonging to the same Church in this district. There was, however, something more than blindly following the lead of the clergy of his Church because they all knew that he held very strong Liberal opinions I and it had always given him a spirit of independence, his action being based on his earnest convictions. As one who had to use licensed houses in all parts of the country, he considered it was absolutely necessary that we should have hotels, and he would not go as far as his friends Mr. Matthews and Mr. Guest, who were honestly of opinion that it would be better for the country if all the licensed premises were closed. Properly conducted licensed houses were in a great measure a necessity while English society was con- stituted as at present. At the same time he could see great danger in the indiscriminate use of public-houses. A large number of people abused the use of strong drink and it would be far better if some limit were placed upon their convenience for getting it. He could not see confiscation or robbery in any part of the Licensing Bill. There was no doubt that in the 1904 Act a free- hold was given to lioensees,but if the Government could give it had also a right to take away. Confiscation was to take something away violently, but the Government had no such intention. Seven years was the longest time limit which had previously been mentioned, and surely fourteen years should be quite sufficient. Person- ally, in order to settle the question, if it could not be done otherwise, he would agree to a time-limit of twenty-one years. As a member of one of the largest clubs in London, he believed it was right that the clubs should have to pay for licences like any other licence-, holder.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Vre.ATTEIEWS moved: "That this meeting held under the auspices of the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League gives its hearty and emphatic support to the Government Licensing Bill." He said that if the Bill was intended in any shape or form to do the smallest measure of injustice even to a great trade like the brewing trade he would not he present to support anything in the way of Iconflscation I or robbery. He supported the measure first because it was strictly just and equitable to the brewer and even generous. Mr. Asquith specifically declared that the Bill was intended to reduce the over-multiplied facilities for strong drink and to restore what had been given to the brewers back to the people. Therefore those men I in the Parr Hall last week who said it would not help I temperance forward one bit were on the wrong tack altogether. It was never intended to be a teetotal Bill; it was never intended to be a temperance measure parse. It was a political Bill—an attempt to restore what had been given to one trade into the hands of those to whom it rightly belonged. All the same, if it reduced ! facilities for drinking, it must touch some of those I people who had no command of themselves, I and if it only made temperance men indirectly it would be a remarkably good measure. (Applause). ; The time limit of fourteen years was an act of more than generosity to the trade. Moreover the Bill was i ethically just. It considered the person whom Mr. I Balfour did not consider at all in 1904—the ordinary licence holder. He wished it had been possible for the I men who had put their little all into licensed houses I belonging to the brewers and who had been unscrupul- ously discharged, to come on to that platform and give I their experiences of the way they had been treated by I the brewers. The Act of 1904 compensated the brewer' but left it optional as to whether the publican should Ibe compensated. Now Mr. Asquith's Bill seriously con- sidered the publican and the brewers would not have a j right to exercise an option at all. He ventured to I think this unscrupulous trade had brought pressure to ! bear on the licence holders of the town, for he believed I that if they could give their honest opinion they would say that the 13111 was an eminently sane and just and equitable one from their point of view. The Bill was also fair to the ratepayers ; for the compensation would not be haphazard, as hitherto, but it would be paid on the value of the house at the time the licence I was taken away. Except in those towns where I stage thunder was being manufactured by the trade,they , found that all through the country opinion was steadily I growing in favour of the Bill. He was looking for- ward to the time when, with the help of the Bishops, the Bill would pass through the House of Lords—Lord Burton and men of that ilk notwithstanding—and I would be happily registered on the statute book, and I when the people of England would once more be able not only to enjoy their own possession of the licences but would be sober, prosperous and even more industrious. (Applause.) Mr. E. J. GussT seconded. He said the measure was not really a Liberal measure. It was a mere accident that a Liberal Government had brought it forward. The Bill had been evolved during many years, and many had been the pioneers who bad been working for that Bill or a similar one. The Bill was really the outcome of the Royal Commission over which Lord Peel presided. It bad enlisted the support of representatives of all shades of opinion and it was therefore an inter-political measure and not a measure of any party politics what- ever. Two events had happened which had put a feel- ing of triumph into the hearts of" the trade." One was the election at Peckham and the other was the appearance on their platform in Warrington of the local clergy. (" Shame.") They were sorry that both those events had taken place, but they were not discouraged by either of them. They would rather be defeated at Peckham than gain the victory by the means which their opponents did. (Applause.) They deeply regretted that the clergy</p>
        <p>who were put in high places in this town for the ethical, moral, and spiritual elevation of the people, should have joined issue with this great trade on the question. They regretted it on the clergy's own account, for they would lose respect and influence in the town for many years to come. The clergy might have discussed the question on its merits. If they objected to any part of it they might have said so, but to have gone bodily over to the enemy was a sad thing indeed. Those who were promoting the measure were charged with breaking the eighth Commandment—men like the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bishop of Liverpool had that charge levelled against them. He put it down to one of three reasons—self-interest, ignorance, and prejudice. They all knew that with the trade it was self-interest only. He did not charge the clergy with being self-interested, but he thought there were indications of ignorance on their part with regard to the Bill. One rev, gentleman preached on " Love" the other day, and he said the Bill did not indicate love on the part of the Government—towards the brewer, he (Mr. Guest) supposed. And yet when the preacher enumerated a number of things he would like to see he (Mr. Guest) found that almost all those reforms were incorporated in the Bill—(applause)—which indicated to him that the rev. gentleman had not read the Bill or had not understood it. Another rev, gentleman was so prejudiced against the Government on account of their Education Bill that he brought the two measures together and did not see anything satisfactory in either of them. The two Bills were absolutely distinct and ought to be treated on their own merits. (Applause). He was sorry to find that at the Parr Hall meeting they brought their speakers virtually from the Police Cousts. These barristers had the power of sophistry, the power of saying black was white and of throwing dirt into the eyes of the people. If the promoters of the meeting had really believed in their trade why could they not have left it to Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. Carleton and the ex-member for the borough to show the goodness of their cause ? He was also sorry the trade was trying to tyrannise over many people. The supporters of the Bill might be scotched for a time, but he did not think they would be for long. He certainly thought the Bill would pass the House of Commons, and if the Bishops had more power than Mr. Balfour it would go through the House of Lords. (Applause.) But if it did not pass through the House of Lords this time it would eventually be, carried. It was the wish of the most enlightened of the people, and of all social reformers that it should be so. There was really no injustice in the measure—in fact it dealt out justice to- the people and also to the licensed victuallers. (Applause.) Mr. JoHN MAGINN, in supporting, said he was not there as a teetotaller or temperance reformer, or as the mouthpiece of any party. He proceeded to criticise the tied-house system, pointieg out that after forming limited companies, the brewers began an agitation to collar all the houses. They were not content to remain manufacturers of intoxicants receiving the ordinary manufacturers' profit, but they must become retailers also. The result was that the old type of host had largely disappeared, and the public had to drink just the kind of beer which the brewers chose to sell them at the brewers' own prices. The brewer did not pay revenue to the State or to the local rates,but he insisted on making the working man pay it through his glass of beer. Speaking of the low assessment of licensed houses as compared with other places of business, Mr. Maginn pointed out that the Roe Buck Hotel, Bridge- street, was rated at only .£93 10s. per year whereas the Public Benefit Boot Company's shop which occupied probably only about a fiftu of the ground was rated at £159 10s. According to that assessment the value of the Roe Buck Hotel at twenty years' purchase wouldlbe £lB7oand that of the Public Benefit Company's shop £3lBO. The Licensing Bill was not all that he would like it it to be. • He would be sorry to see this great country turned into a nation of water drinkers but he would emphasize the necessity of having the best beer and that would only be obtained by competition. He would therefore be inclined to let the brewers work out their own salvation on Mr. Balfour's scheme, but make it a condition that no more licences should be granted to a monopoly, but that the man whose name was over the door should be the actual owner. (Applause.) The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK also supported, and said they were face to face with one of the greatest moral struggles that any generation had witnessed. They must insist on the rights not merely of property but of men. They said distinctly that the trade was unsocial in that it was not ready to give its strength to protect the weak. The Licensing Bill was an effort to make the public-house the social institution which it ought to be. A public-house properly conducted served a good end, and in the present state of 'English life it was necessary. The gin palace was not, however, necessary, and he drew a very strong distinctian between the two places. Why he specially entered into the fight was because of its moral issues. If they did not get the Bill through they would not be able to get another licensing law for whole generation, because the trade would entrench itself more and more behind Parliament and the country would be more and more dictated to instead of being able to say "Thus far shalt thou go and no farther.' (Applause.) The resolution was carried with one dissentient. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman, on the proposition of Mr. AI A.GINN, seconded by Mr. J. G RIFFITIIS.</p>
        <p>NEXT FRIDAY'S GREAT MEETING. We would remind our readers that a great meeting promoted by the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League in support of the Bill will be held in the Parr Hall next Friday evening. The principal speakers will be the Bishop of Liverpool, Mr. C. Roberts, M.P. (Lincoln), and Mr. W. Madden. Mr. Roberts is One of the first authorities in the country on monopoly value and the question of a time limit, and is the author of several valuable contributions to the literature on Licensing Reform. Mr. James Dodds (president of the league) will preside, and will be supported by a body of temperance reformers representing all shades of political and religious opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>ANOTHER LIBERAL WARD MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER LIBERAL WARD MEETING.</p>
        <p>Another meeting promoted by the Liberal Association in support of the Bill will be held in Brick-street Mission on Monday evening. Mr. W. Roberts will preside and the speakers will be Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Mr. J. Magian, 111 r. J. Upson, and Mr. J. Howes.</p>
        <p>RESOLUTIONS. An a public meeting held on Thursday in connection with the Warrington "Y " branch National British Women's Temperance Association the following resolu- tion in support of the Government's Licensing Bill was passed : That this meeting of the Warrington " Y " branch National Woman's Temperance Association fig desires to expre . earty appreciation of the Govern- ments Licensin ,all. It rejoices to see that the Bill includes Local 'etc. for England and Wales, Restora- tion of the Powers of Licensing Magistrates, Abolition of Appeal to Quarter Sessions, and a Time Limit of 14 years. It would respectfully impress upon the Govern- ment the desirability of amending clause 19 so that the exclusion of all children under 14 from drinking-bars should be statutory, and universal. At a meeting held at the Baptist Chapel, Latchford, Warrington, on Sunday evening, March 22nd, 1908, the following resolution was passed :—" That this meeting rejoices in the introduction of the Licensing Bill now before Parliament.that it expresses its hearty agreement with its principles and earnestly hopes that notwith- standing the tremendous opposition which is being and will be engineered to prevent the measure from passing, the promoters of the Bill will maintain an unflinching courage so that it may speedily become the law of the land.</p>
        <p>This meeting further pledges itself to do all in its power to support the Government , the present crusade against the crying evils of tne liquor traffic, which by the introduction of the Licensing Bill the Government in the interests of righteon3ness, sobriety, and National prosperity, has entered."</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-04_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>BREWERS' FUNDS. -- Under Home Secretary's mg Figures.</head>
        <p>BREWERS' FUNDS. -- Under Home Secretary's mg Figures.</p>
        <p>Reassuring Investors. Speaking at Hull the other day, Mr. Herbert Samuel, M.P., Under-Secretary for the Home Department,. referring to the Licensing Bill, said he had obtained the balance-sheets of most of the brewery companies, and he found that they had in hand £13,500,000. In fifteen years that sum, at compound Interest at 4 per cent., will have reached about £24,500,000. Last year these brewery companies added to their reserve no less than £2,750,000. In fifteen years that, money, wisely invested, would yield £5,500,0u0 more. But that was not all. The Trade was paying the compensation levy under the 1904 Act, amounting to over a million a year. In fifteen years, that would reach without interest, £1.6,500,000. It was generous estimate to say that the brewers would get £11,000,000 of that. Therefore, he found, putting the items together, that, without the Trade being called upon to pay a single sixpence more than it is now paying, or without putting a single sixpence more for reserve funds, they would have at the end of the time-limit no less than £41,000,000. These facts, when generally known, would reassure investors in brewery companies, and show them that the time-limit wauld not be such a terrible thing, after all.</p>
