        <p>TOWN COUNCIL. Budget Night. At its monthly meeting on Tuesday the Ton '1 will consider the estimates of the various committee; and fix the rates for the ensuing year. MORE CONSTAI3LES. The Watch Committee recommends the appointment of seven additional constables,and the Chief Constable's report. HOUSING REFORM AND TOWN PLANNING. The National Housing Reform Council having invited I delegates from the Council to attend a Conference on the proposed Bill to amend the law relating to the Housing of the working classes, and to provide for the making of town planning schemes, &amp;c., the Street Improvement Committee has resolved that the Caairman, Deputy-Chairman and Councillor Pemberton be appointed to attend the Conference. DR, GAFFIKIS'S RESIGNATION. Dr. Gaffikin has tendered her resignation to the Health Committee, having been appointed Medical Officer to the Enfield Education Committee. The Health Committee recommends the appointment of an assistant medical officer at a salary of £125. MEDICAL INSPECTION OF SCHOOL CHILDREN. The Health Committee has set apai t the suns of £3OO to proa id e for the above next year. A deputation from the Education Committee explained that they did not propose to proceed any further with the appointment of a medics l officer end nurse. STANDARD RATE OF WAGES. The Paving and Sewerage Committee, in response to a letter from the Tradesmen's Association had resolved that Messrs. Lotery and Co. be requested to inform this committee whether they are paying the standard rate of wages in their district. Messrs. Lotery and Co. have replied as follows : "Tender for slops. In reply to your letter of the 10th instant, we beg to inform you that we pay the standard rate of wages, and observe the hours of labour recog- nised in the trade, and are complying with the terms and conditions of our tender in every respect. A personal visit is invited to our factory, which is at all times open for your inspection." THE BATTIS. 'The Baths Committee have resolved that they are unable to make any arrangements for the attendance of school girls. Resolved that the tender of Mr. William Winstanley for the painting of the inside and outside of the baths according to specification, at ,2172 55., be accepted. .THE GAS ENGINEER'S SALARY. The Gas Committee recommend that the salary of Mr W. S. Haddock, the Gas Engineer, be increased from ' £5OO to £6OO per annum • and that of Mr. Mc Nicholl , his assistant, from £l5O to £175. SMALL HOLDINGS AND ALLOTMENTS. The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee having offered the field adjoining the canal at the Old Warps for the temporary use of persons requiring allotments, resolved, that the offer be accepted. Resolved, that an application from Air. W. B. Hughes for an allotment be granted. That the rent to be paid for allotments be at the rate of 17s. per annum for one- eighth of an acre, payable quarterly. That notice be put up near to the entrance to Victoria Park, stating the number and rents of the vacant allotments. That tenders be obtained for the erection of tool horses and shelters. THE RECENT IRREGULARITIES. The General Purposes Committee's minutes contain a reference to a report from the Joint Committ( appointed to inquire into the recent irregularities in the Borough Surveyor's Department which was sub- mitt( 3, and the consideration thereof deferred until next mc ating. WARRINGTON CHILD'S DEATH. "Father Deserving of Censure." Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at Warrington on Tuesday, touching the death of John Mannion, aged five years, son of Thomas Mannion, labourer, 58, Dallam - lane. Mr. Browne appear.d for the N.S.P.C.C., and Superintendent Monaghan was present on behalf of the police. I Inspector Coy, of the National Society for the ' Prevention of Cruelty to Children, said that in cease • queuce of a report he received he visited the honse of John Mannion on Saturday, March 21st. On going , upstairs, in one of the bedrooms he found two children, I John and Ellen Marnion, aged six and thirteen respectively, lyiag on the bed. The bedding was I filthy, and the smell in the room was sickly. The boy I was in a bad state of health and in an emaciated con- I dition. There was no food in the house. He immedi- ately sent for Dr. Hodgkinsor, and on his advice the children were removed to the workhouse infirmary, where the boy died the following day. The house was quite devoid of comforts. In reply to the Coroner, w itness stated that the I father was not. addicted to drink, but he thought he was lazy. Dr. Hodgkinson said he had made a post-mortem examination, and found the body emaciated and the skin covered with insect bites. The cause of death was pulmonary tuberculous peritonitis.. The Coroner : Are you prepared to say that any act I of commission or omission on the part of the father has brought about death or hastened or accelerated it ? Dr. Hodgkinson : It is a very difficult question to answer. If a person absolutely neglects a child it is almost certain to die sooner. The Coroner.—Supposing the father was sent for trial for manslaughter, and the learned counsel asked you if you were prepared to say positively that this child wonld have lived longer but for the act of omission the part of the father ? Witness : No ; I should believe, but would not say it. The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence, and added that the father was guilty of censmable neglect. The Coroner said that, as the father had to come before the local magistrate, any words that he said might prejudice his case, and if that had not been so he would have said something which would have astop,shed him. FATHER SENT TO GAOL. At the Borough Police Court, on Thursday, 'Thomas , Mannino, of Dallarn-lane, was summoned for neglecting I his three children--Mr. A. Browne' who prosecuted, said the prisoner had been remanded since lass 'Thurs- day on account of the death of one of the children. Since then a coroner's inquest had been held, and the jury returned a verdict that whilst prisoner was not guilty of culpable negligence he was guilty of negli- gence, which ought to be censured. The case was a very sad one, as prior to the death of the prisopar's wife the children were fairly well looked after. After her death the children had been grossly neglected, and prisoner was to blame for not obtaining assistance.—The Chair- man, in sending prisoner to gaol for one month, said he had had a near escape of being charged with manslaughter. Kept:the Dog Quiet At the Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday, before Mr. Henry Roberts, Charles Vincent was charged with breaking and entering the dwelling- house of Mr. Fred Pearson, Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath, between 0 15 and 8 o'clock on Sunday night.—lt was stated that Mr. Pearson locked up the house at 6.15, and, on returning at 8 o'clock, be dis- covered that some one had entered, that several drawers had been ransacked, and that some cooked meat had been given to the dog to keep him quiet in the dining-room. On making a a search upstairs it was found that a gold watch, belonging to Mrs. Pearson and a gold ring belonging to Mr. Pearson, as well as a bunch of keys were missing. The matter was reported to the police,end information circulated in the district, On Monday P.C. White, of High Leigh, received a complaint that a man had stolen twc pennyworth of onions from a grocer's cert. White went in pursuit, and worked his way to Broom Edge, where he met the prisoner on the highroad. The officer took him to Lyinm Police Station, and on being searched the \property stolen from Mr. Pearsm's house was fooml n his possession.—Tha case was remanded for a week.</p>
