        <p>. St. John's Sale of Work. I Following the week of special services in St. John's 1 Presbyterian Church, by the Rev. G. Eskine Nicol, of Withington, a sale of work on behalf of the Church funds, was opened by Mrs. Nicol on Thursday after- noon in the presence of a fair number of workers and friends. Mrs. Nicol, introduced by the Minister, the 1 Rev. J. L. Milne, was accorded a hearty vote of thanks I for her presence and assistance, on the motion of the IRev' Harvey Cook of Cairo-street. I SIX GUINEAS will purchase a fully guaranteed Ij, PREMIER CYCLE. Inspection invited.—J. S. GAR- AND, 51,53, &amp; 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Y Branch, N.B.W.T.A. I A drawing room meeting in connection with the above was held in the Town Hall on Thursday after- noon. The Mayoress presided and a very interesting address was given by Mrs. Randolph Clarkson,General Y. Secretary, of Birmingham. Mrs. Moore also spoke. In the evening a public meeting was held in Wycliffe Memorial Hall, at which Mrs. Dodds presided, when a national demonstration in costume was given by the following Y members: - America,Miss W. Cox; Japan, Miss Lloyd Jones ; Canada, Mrs. Tanner ; Australia, Miss Jackson ; Scotland, Miss Dodds; and England, Miss J. Blackstock. Introductory songs were sung by Miss J. Blackstock, Miss W. Cox, and Miss Lloyd Jones. Mrs. Dodds proposed a resolution agreeing with the Licensing Bill, and Mrs. Randolph Clarkson, in a most eloquent speech, seconded it. The motion was carried unanimously. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 9;icl. &amp; 1/li. Of Cbemists.—Advt</p>
