        <p>; INSTANT RELIEF, RAPID CURE of INSTANT RELIEF RUGBY FOOTBALL. RAPID CURE of GOUT AND RHEUMATISM by GOUT AND RHEUMATISM by EADE'S PILLS. THE NORTHERN UNION CUP. ILI -1-1 , ''S PILLS. 4.• THE SWELLING DISAPPEARED ! ! THIRD ROUND. *Hull *Leigh 1 2 8 Merthyr Tydvil 1 0 2 November 30th, 190 T. Hunslet 1 2 8 *Barrow 0 0 0 5, Grafton-street, Hanley, Staffs. Dear Sir.—During the past six months we have *Broughton 11 Sir —.l think it is my duty to write and tell you whilst hoisting in and out ooean tugs, copper punts of the great benefit I that have received from your and other small crafts, I contracted several chills THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. Gout and Rheumatic Pills. I have been a great one after the other, which finally settled in my legs *Leeds taken all kinds of remedies without any benefit. I *Brantley ... ....... .. 1 1 5 Ebbw Vale *Halifax *Runcorn *Dewsbury -- NEVER WITHOUT A BOTTLE IN your advertisement, I determined to try your EXHIBITION MATCH.—At Kensal Rise. 1 THE HOUSE. famous Goutg'ills, and after taking a few doses Huddersfield - they worked marvels. All the swelling disappeared and my legs are as strong as ever again. 1 2 8 • ment, which is strictly true. Warrington Die Hard at Broughton. Yours Respectfully, G. SWACKENHAMMER. Q.G. Warrington said good-bye to the Northern Union Cup ours respectfully, THOMAS RATBBONE, EADE'S GOUT PILLS ! bhi yl oydtrestorative. dp 0213 i dri eeo EADE'S GC.)UT PILLS on Saturday at Broughton, but they did not give up possession until after a very hard struggle. They were Are perfectly safe in their action, eliminate all Are perfectly safe in their action, eliminate all unlucky in having to finish the game with only 12 men, injurious matter, and are highly restorative. everywheref ir ae u se for matter,st ai ran pallßsodottarrlepes.l,loli a fact which had some bearing on the result. The Sold everywhere in bottle Is. lid.and 2s. 9d. or sent oplodsj match was favoured by beautiful weather, and the post free for stamps or .0. by the proprietor,rrsS,ent Wheater's Field ground was thronged by a crowd of GEORGE EADE, 232, Goswell road London GEORGE EADE, 2324G05we1l road Lorden. nearly 22.000 people, about a quarter of whom had come from Warrington. The Cheshire Lines trains 1.0.5 'ADE'S GOUT &amp; RHEUMATIC PILLS were packed by local enthusiasts, and primrose and blue . favours were much in evidence. Both teams turned out as selected, hut while the Rangers were at full strength Warrington were without Tilley at full back, and experimented with Isherwood on the wing. Such men as Lees and Jenkins were, of course, not available. It was therefore not quite the Warrington club's full strength whish took the field. Broughton being the first to enter the field were given a great reception, but even this was dwarfed when the Cup-holders stepped into the arena. The same dis- gusting order of things which characterised the game at Runcorn in the shape of a coursing match with the greyhound bearing the Warrington colours was again introduced, but this time the rabbit did not move, and fell an easy prey. It is to be hoped that the name of the Warrington F.C. is not going to be associated with this travesty of sport. The teams and officials were : Warrington : Jordan, back ; Fish, Dickenson Taylor , and Isherwood, three-quarter backs ; Lloyd and 'Brooks, half backs ; Shugars, Jenkins, Thomas, Belton, P. Boardman and Naylor, forwards. Broughton Rangers : Barlow, back ; Hardyman, Wilson, Hogg, and C. James, three-quarter backs ; S. James and Flynn, halt backs ' • Ruddick, Beetham, Clampitt, Darlison, Winskill and Grainey, forwards. Referee, Mr. H. Farrar, Keighley ; touch-judges, Messrs. G. Moore, Runcorn, and J. T. Hayes, Leigh. Warrington won the toss and playing with a slight breeze behind them defended the Broughton goal. Ruddick was deputed to start the game of eighty minutes duration. and Taylor, meeting the initial kick, the game settled at mid-field. Both sets of forwards immediately threw whole-hearted energy into their work, and early the tackling was of a robust nature. The Rangers' backs were getting the oval frequently from the pack, and Wilson tried to make an opening for Hardyman, but Fish prevented the ex-Leicester man from making headway and rushed him into touch. For a time the game went in favour of the home men but a couple of penalties against the Rangers for obstruc- tive tactics gave Warrington the attack, and now that , they had taken up a short spell in the Broughton , quarters, the forwards appeared to control the ball better at close quarters. Lloyd failed badly when he kicked beyond S. James and Hogg when passing to the rear line would have paid better and Barlow easily cleared. Hogg tried to make headway, only to see Jenkins field magnificently, and Fish subsequently try to land a goal from a rather long range, the ball falling under the bar. The Broughton vanguard replied and Jenkins in stopping a rush was damaged and assisted to the touch line. This was a serious loss to the visitors, for the Welsh- man, although he bravely took the field again, was little use, and in the second moiety was compelled to retire. From the pack which followed Lloyd again got away and kicked to Barlow, and Fish following up at top speed tackled the home custodian in possession. Then mime a chance to the Warrington men which ought to have realized something substantial, for Dickenson, taking advantage of some loose play by the home backs dashed down the open and handed on his charge to Fish. That player tricked Hardyman and with a clear opening the War- ringtonians looked certain to score, when he slipped and before he could recover his feet Barlow seized him firmly and the situation was saved. A lucky escape by Barlow was followed by a hasty raid to the Warrington end, where Wilson had a futile shot at goal. Warring- ton retaliated, and after Lloyd and Brooks had handled the sphere P. Boardman threw the ball to Jenkins, who was struggling hard despite his injury, but was perforce compelled to part with the ball, and a forward transfer to Fish again spoiled Warrington of an excellent chance. Judicious kicking by S. James and Flynn gave Warrington a fright, for immediately after the Rangers made a desperate effort to cross, but the defence held out, and Taylor and Dickenson were to the fore in changing the venue. The visiting forwards again rushed the game to the quarter flag and Brooks breaking splendidly away held the oval just a shade too long when Taylor was well placed for a try. The chance was lost, and immediately after the whistle went for half-time with the score sheet blank. Thomas restarted and the Warringtonians had a splendid opportunity of taking the lead in the first minute, for Taylor in following up a kick was on the top of Barlow when the Broughton custodian misflelded the sphere, but the Warrington centre failed to take the chance and the Rangers had a remarkable escape. Dickenson next tried to break through a strong cordon of Broughton forwards without success, being held up on the line, whilst a round of passing gave Fish an opportunity, and though he succeeded in dogging several pairs of anxious arms the effort proved too much for him and he was tackled a yard inside. Flynn got his side out of a very tight corner and tkis relief was the forerunner to a Broughton success, for a penalty against the visitors near the half-way line was successfully taken by Claude James, and Warring- ton were two points in arrears after the game had been in progress just an hour. Warrington immediately , rushed the ball to the Broughton end on resuming, and a futile effort by Dickenson was followed by Isherwood being tackled close to the line by Barlow. Broughton raised the siege once more and a round of passing by the whole of the backs ended in Hardyman racing over at the corner. The Rangers now continued to attack and several times their backs looked dangerous, Fish being especially to the fore in saving his line. Seven minutes from the finish Claude James dropped a capital goal. In the last few minutes Warrington rallied splendidly, and Fish kicked a penalty kick and recorded Warrington's only tangible points. A chance came to the visitors immediately after when Lloyd, Brooks and Taylor got working on the right, and it was not until Barlow effectively tackled Brooks that damage was averted. Play was of the safety order, and Broughton at the close had won a strenuous struggle. Result : Broughton Rangers, two goals, one try (seven points) ; Warrington, one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. SOLD EVERYIVIIBBB at Is. lid., Es. ad.,44.6,1., an 4 /1.. • The Vacant Appointments. 0 A meeting of the Warrington Board of ns Guardia was Yrbridge held at tho Workhouse on Wednesday under the LL TH* presidency Houghton. There roaalsdo. LU N GNTO NIC 9 , Wnsi n's FOR COUGHS, COLDS, BRONCHITIS, AST HMA, CiME A bent, Revs. T. R. Matthews and M. Ryan, Dr. Murray, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. Billinge, W. Gill, R. Bolton, W. Furness, W. C. Brooks, .L Cook, G. Mather, W. Flannagan, M. W. Seton, A. Brooks, W. Stanier, J. Evans, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master) and A. Wright (superintendent of Cottage Homes). THE VACANT APPOINTMENTS. 0 APPLICATIONS TO BE CONSIDERED BY THE WHOLE LAREts .zs BOAILD. ERE The CLERK reported that the special committee VLUUU /t acting re the vacancies for Clerk and Assistant Clerk recommended (as in the recent cases of medical vacancies) that the Clerk be empowered to appoint a temporary assistant for the period from March 25th to June 24th at a remuneration not exceeding the rate paid to the late assistant clerk per quarter. He had agreed to employ Mr. E. J. Sutton, who was well acquainted with the work, for the period of vacancy. He had by direction of the committee issued an advertisement inviting applications for the appointments, the salaries affered being £250 for the clerk and £lOO for the assistant clerk. It was intended that the applications should be considered by the committee on Wednesday, April Bth, at 2 p.m. at the Rural District Council offices. ' Mr. BILLINGE moved and Mr. MATHER seconded the confirmation of this report. Mrs. WrrxrxsoN moved an amendment that the whole Board should consider the appllcations. The Board had not a say in the fixing of the salaries, the advertisement being issued before most of the members knew. It was not fair to the Board, and she moved that they should all consider the appointments, instead of a small committee. The CHAIRMAN : The Board appointed the com- mittee with full powers to deal with the matter. Mrs. WILKINSON: I did not know that they had to advertise before reporting to the Board. Mr. Srros : The Committee was elected to deal , with the appointment of assistant clerk. We did not know anything then about Mr. J. C. Sutton going to ' resign. The CLERK said that although there might be con- venience in a committee opening the applications first, he would recommend as a matter ,of policy if not of law, that the whole of the applications should come before the Board in some such way as would enable the , Guardians generally to see them. The CHAIRMAN : One of the members of the Com- mittee has become a candidate for one of the °glees and has resigned his place on the Committee. Mr. SETON seconded Mrs. Wilkinson's amendment. In reply to Mr. A. Brook's the CLERK said there had already been over 200 applications for forms (75 for the clerkship and 130 for the assistant clerkship.) Mr. A. BROOKS sympathised with Mrs. Wilkinson's ; idea but thought it was unworkable. The Board should be able to trust the Committee to select three or four candidates to appear before them. On a vote being taken Mrs. Wilkinson's amendment , was carried by 8 votes to 7. Consequently all the , Guardians will be entitled to be present at the opening) of the applications next Wednesday. APPLICATION FOR SPECIAL PAY. The CLERK read an application from Messrs. Awbery (master), \Wright (superintendent of the Cottage Homes), Garnett, Bowes and Aukland (relieving officers), for extra remuneration for the preparation of the relief lists as required by the Local Government Board. It was decided to ask each applicant to present a report on the work he ha. done. ILLNESS OF DR. PATTERSON. Mr. BILLINGE expressed great regret that Dr. Patterson was lying seriously ill and on his proposition it was derided to send an expression of sympathy.</p>
